var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"title":"API Reference","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#API-Reference","category":"section","text":"Every exported name of CategoricalBayesianNetworks.jl and InfluenceDiagrams.jl is re-exported, among them the Catlab names those packages re-export (apex, legs, feet, dom, codom, compose, otimes, munit, id, oapply, @relation) and every exception type of the lower packages, as the same bindings. Those names are documented by their packages. This page documents the names this package defines, and the methods it adds to the re-exported functions: among them compose, otimes, dom, codom, isapprox and kernel on a RewardKernel, interpret on an open influence diagram, and optimize on a closed composite."},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams","category":"module","text":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams\n\nOpen influence diagrams as structured cospans (ADR 0018). This is the decision-layer analogue of CategoricalBayesianNetworks.jl: it adds to InfluenceDiagrams.jl, which stays Catlab-free (ADR 0009), the open-system machinery of the categorical layer.\n\nOpen diagrams (src/open.jl): OpenInfluenceDiagram, a Catlab structured cospan with an InfluenceDiagram apex and VariableSpace feet, the feet being the same type as those of an open Bayesian network. Every interface variable is also declared :observed (remembered information a later decision may read) or :hidden. The typed-interface rule of open Bayesian networks is extended to decisions and utilities and checked by validation_errors. An OpenBayesNet embeds with every interface variable :hidden.\nComposition (src/composition.jl): compose, glue, otimes and oapply, each a pushout or coproduct of ACSets, with the rule (including its no-forgetting clause) checked again on the composite.\nSemantics for a fixed strategy (src/semantics.jl): RewardKernel, the expectation-semiring kernel category over FiniteKernels.jl, and interpret, which maps an open influence diagram and a deterministic strategy to its reward kernel, functorially for compose and otimes.\nOptimisation, contextual and never isolated (src/optimization.jl): optimize on a closed composite, and contextual_value and contextual_optimize on an open component against an explicit boundary prior and continuation value, with continuation_value and boundary_law the contexts a serial composite induces.\nReward certificates (src/certificates.jl, ADR 0019): export_reward_certificate writes a diagram, a strategy and the reward kernel interpret computed for it as an OpenInfluence.RewardCertificate/v1 record, which the Lean checker in proofs/ reads.\n\nEvery exported name of CategoricalBayesianNetworks and InfluenceDiagrams is re-exported, so using CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams is a superset of using both.\n\nPart of the ecorecipes compositional Bayesian-network ecosystem.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.OBSERVABILITY","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.OBSERVABILITY","category":"constant","text":"OBSERVABILITY\n\nThe two observability flags of an interface variable (ADR 0018, decision 2): :observed, remembered information that a later decision may read, and :hidden, state that crosses the boundary but that no decision on the far side may read.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalBayesianNetworks.OpenBayesNet","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalBayesianNetworks.OpenBayesNet-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"OpenBayesNet(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram) -> OpenBayesNet\n\nThe open Bayesian network underlying o, which must have no decisions, utilities or precedence rows (ArgumentError otherwise, naming them): the apex is BayesNet(apex(o)), whose parts keep their ids, and the feet are those of o. Observability flags are dropped; in a diagram without decisions no variable is read as information. This inverts OpenInfluenceDiagram(o::OpenBayesNet).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.DiagramInterfaceError","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.DiagramInterfaceError","category":"type","text":"DiagramInterfaceError(rule, variable, by, kind)\n\nA violation of the typed-interface rule of open influence diagrams (ADR 0018, decision 2) that BayesianNetworks and InfluenceDiagrams have no type for:\n\nrule 2, an input-foot variable has a generator in the apex: input variable is generated by the mechanism (kind = :mechanism) or the decision (kind = :decision) named by;\nrule 3, a decision reads a hidden input: decision by has variable, an input-foot variable declared :hidden, in its information set (kind = :hidden_input).\n\nThe other rules are reported by the lower packages' types: rule 1 by DuplicateGeneratorError and DecisionUniquenessError, rule 4 (a utility scope reads an apex variable) by DanglingReferenceError, rule 5 by CyclicBayesNetError, InvalidInformationSetError and DecisionPrecedenceCycleError, and rule 6 by NoForgettingError.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.DuplicatePolicyError","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.DuplicatePolicyError","category":"type","text":"DuplicatePolicyError(decision)\n\nmerge_strategies received two strategies that both have a policy for the decision named decision. The strategy of a composite is the disjoint union of its components' strategies (ADR 0018, decision 3), so each decision name may come from one component only.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.InfluenceDiagramCospan","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.InfluenceDiagramCospan","category":"type","text":"InfluenceDiagramCospan\n\nInfluenceDiagramCospanUntyped{Symbol, Int, KernelRef}: a Catlab structured (multi)cospan whose apex is an InfluenceDiagram and whose feet are VariableSpaces, the analogue of OpenBayesNet. structured_cospan returns the one an OpenInfluenceDiagram carries.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.InfluenceDiagramCospanOb","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.InfluenceDiagramCospanOb","category":"type","text":"InfluenceDiagramCospanOb\n\nInfluenceDiagramCospanObUntyped{Symbol, Int, KernelRef}: a VariableSpace wrapped as a Catlab StructuredCospanOb, the interface object of the cospan that an OpenInfluenceDiagram carries. It has no observability; the object of the category of open influence diagrams is OpenInfluenceDiagramOb.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.InfluenceDiagramCospanObUntyped","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.InfluenceDiagramCospanObUntyped","category":"type","text":"InfluenceDiagramCospanObUntyped{Label, Position, Ref}\n\nObject type of the structured cospans under an open influence diagram, with attribute types left open (a Catlab StructuredCospanOb wrapping a VariableSpaceUntyped). See InfluenceDiagramCospanOb.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.InfluenceDiagramCospanUntyped","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.InfluenceDiagramCospanUntyped","category":"type","text":"InfluenceDiagramCospanUntyped{Label, Position, Ref}\n\nMorphism type of the structured cospans under an open influence diagram, with attribute types left open (a Catlab StructuredMulticospan with an InfluenceDiagramUntyped apex). See InfluenceDiagramCospan.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.NoForgettingError","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.NoForgettingError","category":"type","text":"NoForgettingError(decision, variable, earlier)\n\nRule 6 of the typed-interface rule (ADR 0018, decision 2): the components of a composite had no-forgetting, but the composite does not. Decision earlier precedes decision in the composite, and variable (in the information set of earlier, or its action) is not in the information set of decision: the arc variable -> decision is missing. Across a boundary this means that variable was not passed on as an :observed interface variable, or that the later component's decision does not read it.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.ObservabilityMismatchError","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.ObservabilityMismatchError","category":"type","text":"ObservabilityMismatchError(variable, position, left, right, context)\n\nTwo interface variables that are glued together agree in name, states and reference but not in observability: one side declares variable :observed (remembered information a later decision may read) and the other :hidden (ADR 0018, decision 2). position is the index in the interface (or in the along list, or the junction) where the difference was found, left and right are the two flags, and context says which operation compared them.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.OpenInfluenceDiagram","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.OpenInfluenceDiagram","category":"type","text":"OpenInfluenceDiagram\n\nAn open influence diagram (ADR 0018, decision 2): a Catlab structured cospan (InfluenceDiagramCospan) whose apex is an InfluenceDiagram and whose two feet are VariableSpaces, together with an OBSERVABILITY flag for every variable of each foot. Build one with Open, or embed an OpenBayesNet with OpenInfluenceDiagram(o::OpenBayesNet); combine with compose, glue, otimes and oapply. Inspect with apex, legs, feet, dom, codom, inputs, outputs, input_space, output_space, input_variables, output_variables, left_leg, right_leg, input_observability, output_observability, observed_inputs, observed_outputs and structured_cospan.\n\nOnly variables cross the boundary: decisions and utilities stay inside their component.\n\nThe inner constructor OpenInfluenceDiagram(cospan, input_observability, output_observability) checks only that there is one flag per foot variable and that each is :observed or :hidden (ArgumentError otherwise); it does not check the typed-interface rule, which validate does.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.OpenInfluenceDiagram","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.OpenInfluenceDiagram-Tuple{Catlab.CategoricalAlgebra.Pointwise.StructuredCospans.StructuredCospan{Catlab.CategoricalAlgebra.Pointwise.StructuredCospans.DiscreteACSet{VariableSpace, BayesNet}}}","category":"method","text":"OpenInfluenceDiagram(o::OpenBayesNet; validate = true) -> OpenInfluenceDiagram\n\nThe open Bayesian network o as an open influence diagram with no decisions and no utilities, and with every interface variable :hidden (ADR 0018, Consequences). The apex is InfluenceDiagram(apex(o)), whose parts keep their ids, and the feet are those of o. OpenBayesNet of the result gives back o. With validate = true the typed-interface rule is checked.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.OpenInfluenceDiagramError","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.OpenInfluenceDiagramError","category":"type","text":"OpenInfluenceDiagramError\n\nAbstract supertype of every exception introduced by CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.jl. It is a subtype of BayesianNetworks' BayesNetError, so e isa AnyBayesNetError catches these errors too.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.OpenInfluenceDiagramOb","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.OpenInfluenceDiagramOb","category":"type","text":"OpenInfluenceDiagramOb(space::VariableSpace, observability::Vector{Symbol})\n\nAn object of the category of open influence diagrams: an interface, that is a VariableSpace together with one OBSERVABILITY flag per variable, in interface order. dom(o) and codom(o) of an OpenInfluenceDiagram return one; its fields space and observability are the interface and its flags. Two objects are equal when their spaces and flags are. Building one with a flag that is not :observed or :hidden, or with a number of flags other than the number of variables, raises ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.RewardCertificateError","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.RewardCertificateError","category":"type","text":"RewardCertificateError(code, path, message)\n\nexport_reward_certificate cannot write an OpenInfluence.RewardCertificate/v1 record (ADR 0019) for the diagram and tables it was given. code names the condition, path locates it in the certificate (dotted keys with 0-based array indices, such as network.mechanisms[1].kernelRef, or the companion key for exact_tables) and message names the parts and variables involved. The codes are:\n\n:unset_reference: a space_ref or kernel_ref has no value; the certificate records every reference (NoRef() records none);\n:unsupported_reference: a reference of a type the format has no tag for (a user-defined KernelRef subtype);\n:policy_reference_on_chance: a chance mechanism carries a PolicyRef, which the format reserves for decisions;\n:inconsistent_decision: two equally named decisions, which share one policy, differ in the number of their information inputs, in those inputs' state counts or in their action's state count;\n:too_many_entries: the certificate would hold more than max_entries cells;\n:non_finite: a cell to be written is not finite;\n:missing_companion, :extra_companion and :companion_rounding: in rational_exact mode, a chance or utility cell has no exact companion, a companion matches no cell, or a companion does not round (to nearest, ties to even) to the Float64 cell it accompanies.\n\nInvalid keyword values raise ArgumentError instead, and structural and semantic failures keep the exceptions of validate and interpret.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.RewardKernel","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.RewardKernel","category":"type","text":"RewardKernel(K::FiniteKernel, W::AbstractArray{<:Real})\nRewardKernel(K::FiniteKernel)\n\nA morphism of the expectation-semiring kernel category (ADR 0018, decision 3): the semantics of an open influence diagram X → Y under a fixed strategy.\n\nK is a FiniteKernel X → Y: K(y | x) is the probability of reaching the output state y from the input state x.\nW is a real table of exactly the shape of K's table, outputs first: W[y..., x...] is the expectation, given x, of the reward earned on the way times the indicator that the output state is y. It may be signed. Summed over the outputs it is the expected reward given x.\n\nWithout W the reward is zero: this lifts a kernel such as an identity, a copy, a discard or a swap into the category. A W of another shape, or with an entry that is not finite, raises ArgumentError.\n\nRead the parts with kernel(r) and reward, the spaces with dom(r) and codom(r). Combine with compose and otimes; the structure maps are identity_reward_kernel, copy_reward_kernel, discard_reward_kernel and swap_reward_kernel. Joint states are ordered as FiniteKernels' joint_states (the first variable varies fastest), so reward_matrix and kernel_matrix(kernel(r)) share their rows and columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AlgebraicInterfaces.compose","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#AlgebraicInterfaces.compose-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram, OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"compose(A::OpenInfluenceDiagram, B::OpenInfluenceDiagram; no_forgetting = true) -> OpenInfluenceDiagram\nA ⋅ B\n\nStrict sequential composition. The output interface of A must equal the input interface of B position by position: names, states with positions and references (BayesianNetworks' InterfaceMismatchError names the first difference), then observability (ObservabilityMismatchError). Both diagrams are validated first. The result is a Catlab pushout of the two apexes along the shared foot, with the inputs of A and the outputs of B and their flags.\n\nThe composite is validated (see validation_errors; with unique names when both pieces had them), then rule 6: when both components have no-forgetting, so must the composite, or a NoForgettingError names the first decision that forgets and the missing information arc. A decision of B that comes after a decision of A has to know what that decision knew and did, and it can only learn it through observed interface variables. Here, decision D_k precedes D_l when a directed path of causal, information or precedence arcs runs from the action of D_k to the action of D_l, and no-forgetting asks that the information set of D_l contain that of D_k and its action. For decisions that such paths order totally this is InfluenceDiagrams' is_no_forgetting. With no_forgetting = false rule 6 is skipped, and the result may be a limited-memory diagram, which ExhaustivePolicySearch solves on the closed composite.\n\nUnset references, utility_ref included, survive the pushout unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AlgebraicInterfaces.compose","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#AlgebraicInterfaces.compose-Tuple{RewardKernel, RewardKernel}","category":"method","text":"compose(a::RewardKernel, b::RewardKernel, cs::RewardKernel...) -> RewardKernel\na ⋅ b\n\nSequential composition in diagrammatic order (first a, then b): (K₁, W₁) ; (K₂, W₂) = (K₁K₂, W₁K₂ + K₁W₂), where K₁K₂ is FiniteKernels' compose_kernel. The reward earned in a is carried through b's kernel and the reward earned in b is weighted by the probability of reaching each of its inputs. Further reward kernels are folded on from the left. codom(a) must equal dom(b) (FiniteKernels' SpaceMismatchError).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AlgebraicInterfaces.dom","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#AlgebraicInterfaces.dom-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"dom(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram) -> OpenInfluenceDiagramOb\ncodom(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram) -> OpenInfluenceDiagramOb\n\nThe input (dom) and output (codom) interface of o, with its observability flags.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AlgebraicInterfaces.dom","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#AlgebraicInterfaces.dom-Tuple{RewardKernel}","category":"method","text":"dom(r::RewardKernel) -> FiniteSpace\ncodom(r::RewardKernel) -> FiniteSpace\n\nThe input and output space of r, those of kernel(r).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AlgebraicInterfaces.id","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#AlgebraicInterfaces.id-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagramOb}","category":"method","text":"id(X::OpenInfluenceDiagramOb) -> OpenInfluenceDiagram\n\nThe identity on the interface X: an apex holding the variables of X and nothing else, with both legs the identity and both feet flagged as in X. Composing with it changes a diagram only up to isomorphism.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AlgebraicInterfaces.munit","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#AlgebraicInterfaces.munit-Tuple{Type{OpenInfluenceDiagramOb}}","category":"method","text":"munit(::Type{OpenInfluenceDiagramOb}) -> OpenInfluenceDiagramOb\n\nThe monoidal unit: the empty interface.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"Base.isapprox","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Base.isapprox-Tuple{RewardKernel, RewardKernel}","category":"method","text":"isapprox(a::RewardKernel, b::RewardKernel; kwargs...) -> Bool\n\nWhether a and b have the same spaces and approximately equal kernels and reward tables; the keywords (atol, rtol) are passed to both comparisons.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"Base.isvalid","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Base.isvalid-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"isvalid(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram; unique_names = false) -> Bool\n\nWhether validation_errors is empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"BayesianNetworks.inputs","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#BayesianNetworks.inputs-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"inputs(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram) -> Vector{Symbol}\noutputs(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nNames of the input and output variables of o, in interface order.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"BayesianNetworks.kernel","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#BayesianNetworks.kernel-Tuple{RewardKernel}","category":"method","text":"kernel(r::RewardKernel) -> FiniteKernel\n\nThe probability part K of the reward kernel r.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"BayesianNetworks.validate","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#BayesianNetworks.validate-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"validate(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram; unique_names = false) -> Nothing\n\nThrow the first entry of validation_errors, if any.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"BayesianNetworks.validation_errors","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#BayesianNetworks.validation_errors-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"validation_errors(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram; unique_names = false) -> Vector{Exception}\n\nAll problems of the open influence diagram o under the typed-interface rule of ADR 0018 (decision 2), without stopping at the first. The structural checks of the apex come first (InfluenceDiagrams' validation_errors, always with closed = false, since an input has no generator); they cover rules 1, 4 and 5:\n\nevery variable has at most one generator, a chance mechanism or a decision (DuplicateGeneratorError, DecisionUniquenessError);\na utility scope may read any apex variable, inputs included; it may not read a variable that is not in the apex (DanglingReferenceError);\nthe causal-plus-information graph is acyclic (CyclicBayesNetError, InvalidInformationSetError with reason :downstream, DecisionPrecedenceCycleError).\n\nThen the interface checks, which are skipped when the apex has a DanglingReferenceError or a MissingAttributeError. The legs must be natural and preserve names, references and states (InterfaceError 5), and the input leg injective (InterfaceError 2), as for an open Bayesian network. Then\n\nan input-foot variable has no generator in the apex (DiagramInterfaceError with rule 2);\nand every variable without a generator is an input (InterfaceError 3, as for an open network: an action variable has a generator, its decision);\na decision's information inputs are internal variables or observed input-foot variables (DiagramInterfaceError with rule 3).\n\nRule 6, that composition preserves no-forgetting, concerns a composite and its components, not one diagram, and is checked by the operations that build composites (see compose). An output variable may be declared observed or hidden freely: whether a later decision may read it is decided by the flag, not by where it came from.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalBayesianNetworks.Open","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalBayesianNetworks.Open-Tuple{InfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"Open(id::InfluenceDiagram; inputs = Symbol[], outputs = Symbol[], observed = Symbol[],\n     validate = true) -> OpenInfluenceDiagram\nOpen(id::InfluenceDiagram, inputs, outputs; observed = Symbol[], validate = true)\n\nMake id an open influence diagram with the named variables as input and output interfaces, in the given order. Each foot is a VariableSpace copying the variables' names, references and states (with positions), exactly as for an OpenBayesNet. Names may repeat in outputs (an implicit copy) but not in inputs.\n\nobserved names the interface variables that are :observed: remembered information that a decision may read. Every other interface variable is :hidden. It takes two forms.\n\nA vector of names. A name in it is observed on every foot it appears on, and a name that is on neither foot raises ArgumentError.\nA named tuple (inputs = [...], outputs = [...]), either field optional (default empty), which flags each foot separately: the names in observed.inputs are observed on the input foot, those in observed.outputs on the output foot. A name in observed.inputs that is not an input, or in observed.outputs that is not an output, raises ArgumentError naming them, as does any other field or an entry that is not a Symbol. The vector [n...] is the named tuple that lists each n on every foot it is on.\n\nThe default is that everything is hidden. It is the conservative choice: a decision may never read a hidden input (rule 3), so an interface carries information only where it says so, and an OpenBayesNet embeds with every variable hidden.\n\nPer-foot flags matter for a pass-through variable, one on both feet:\n\nobserved on the input foot, hidden on the output foot: the boundary forgets the variable. The component's decisions may read it, but a decision downstream cannot, since the next component must take it hidden to match (or ObservabilityMismatchError) and rule 3 then forbids reading it. If such a decision comes after one that read the variable, the composite fails rule 6 (NoForgettingError).\nhidden on the input foot, observed on the output foot: the component reveals the variable, as a perfect observation made inside the component, and a downstream decision may read it. The component's own decisions still may not, since rule 3 is about inputs. Composites are validated again, so a decision downstream still cannot read the variable when a composite's input foot hides it: compose(R, B), with R revealing X to a decision of B that reads it, fails rule 3 on the composite, because X is a hidden input of R ⋅ B; composed after a component that generates X, it is internal and legal.\n\nWith validate = true the typed-interface rule is checked (see validate) and the first violation thrown. An unknown name raises BayesianNetworks' UnknownVariableError.\n\nExample\n\njulia> id = influence_diagram(:Weather => [:sunny, :rainy],\n                              :Forecast => [:sunny, :cloudy, :rainy],\n                              :Umbrella => [:take, :leave];\n                              decisions = [:Umbrella => :Forecast],\n                              utilities = [:U => (:Weather, :Umbrella)], closed = false);\n\njulia> B = Open(id; inputs = [:Weather, :Forecast], observed = [:Forecast]);\n\njulia> inputs(B), input_observability(B)\n([:Weather, :Forecast], [:hidden, :observed])\n\njulia> R = Open(influence_diagram(:X => [:a, :b]; closed = false);\n                inputs = [:X], outputs = [:X], observed = (outputs = [:X],));\n\njulia> input_observability(R), output_observability(R)\n([:hidden], [:observed])\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalBayesianNetworks.glue","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalBayesianNetworks.glue-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram, OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"glue(A::OpenInfluenceDiagram, B::OpenInfluenceDiagram; along, no_forgetting = true) -> OpenInfluenceDiagram\n\nGlue selected outputs of A to selected inputs of B: each a => b in along::Vector{Pair{Symbol,Symbol}} names an output variable of A and an input variable of B, which must carry the same states (positions included), reference and observability, but may differ in name (the glued variable keeps its name in A). BayesianNetworks' InterfaceMismatchError reports a name that is not in the relevant interface, a variable glued twice, or the first pair whose states or references differ, and ObservabilityMismatchError a pair whose flags differ. A rename whose new name is already taken in apex(B) is refused with BayesianNetworks' NameClashError.\n\nThe result has inputs inputs(A) followed by the unglued inputs of B, and outputs the unglued outputs of A followed by outputs(B), each with its flag. It is validated as a composite, rule 6 included, exactly as for compose. Both operands are validated first, as compose validates them. Unlike the open-network glue this matters: rule 3 concerns a component's own input foot, and gluing turns a glued input into an internal variable, so a decision that reads a hidden input of B would otherwise pass on the composite.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalBayesianNetworks.input_space","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalBayesianNetworks.input_space-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"input_space(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram) -> VariableSpace\noutput_space(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram) -> VariableSpace\n\nThe input (left) and output (right) foot of o.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalBayesianNetworks.input_variables","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalBayesianNetworks.input_variables-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"input_variables(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram) -> Vector{Int}\noutput_variables(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram) -> Vector{Int}\n\nApex part ids of the input and output variables, in interface order.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalBayesianNetworks.interface_matches","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalBayesianNetworks.interface_matches-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagramOb, OpenInfluenceDiagramOb}","category":"method","text":"interface_matches(X::OpenInfluenceDiagramOb, Y::OpenInfluenceDiagramOb) -> Bool\n\nWhether two interfaces of open influence diagrams agree exactly: their spaces match in the sense of CategoricalBayesianNetworks' interface_matches (the same variables, states with positions, and references, position by position), and their OBSERVABILITY flags are equal. This is the condition under which compose glues codom(A) to dom(B).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalBayesianNetworks.interpret","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalBayesianNetworks.interpret-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram, AbstractDict, AbstractDict, Strategy}","category":"method","text":"interpret(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram, kernels, utilities, σ::Strategy;\n          max_states = 1_000_000, atol = DEFAULT_ATOL) -> RewardKernel\ninterpret(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram, m::InfluenceDiagramModel, σ::Strategy = strategy(m); kwargs...)\n\nThe semantics ⟦o⟧_σ : ⊗ inputs → ⊗ outputs of an open influence diagram under the fixed deterministic strategy σ (ADR 0018, decision 3), as a RewardKernel (K, W). For each joint input state x, K(y | x) is the probability that the outputs take the joint state y, and W[y..., x...] the expectation of the total (additive) utility of the apex times the indicator that the outputs are y. The inputs have no mechanism, so the apex is conditioned on them: the product of the chance kernels and the policy kernels is summed over every apex variable that is not an output, with the inputs held at x.\n\nThe layout is that of CategoricalBayesianNetworks' interpret for an open network: the input and output spaces have one axis per interface variable, in interface order (an output listed twice is copied, an input that is also an output passes through), and the joint states are enumerated with the first variable fastest. On a closed composite (no inputs, no outputs) the result is the 1 × 1 reward kernel with K = 1 and W the expected utility that InfluenceDiagrams' expected_utility computes on the instantiated diagram.\n\nThe tables come from the apex's references, as instantiate reads them:\n\nkernels is a dictionary keyed by KernelRef or by target variable name, exactly as for CategoricalBayesianNetworks' interpret (MissingKernelError, KernelBindingError);\nutilities a dictionary of AbstractUtilitys keyed by utility name (as bound_utilities of a model) or by utility_ref (MissingUtilityError when one does not resolve, UtilityScopeError when one does not fit its scope);\na model m supplies kernels(m) and bound_utilities(m), and σ defaults to its strategy. A model with evidence raises ArgumentError: evidence composition is outside ADR 0018's first release.\n\nσ needs a DeterministicPolicy or ConstantPolicy for every decision name of the apex (IncompleteStrategyError; a StochasticPolicy raises ArgumentError), checked against the decision's information and action axes (PolicySignatureError); policies for other decisions are ignored, as instantiate ignores them, and decisions that share a name share their policy. A decision that reads an observed input reads that input's state directly: the policy is evaluated at the input state x, which is what rule 3 makes legal. Build the strategy of a composite with merge_strategies.\n\no is validated first. At most max_states joint states of the apex are enumerated (ModelTooLargeError); atol is the normalisation tolerance of the kernels.\n\nFunctoriality, checked numerically by the test suite against the closed-composite oracle: interpret(compose(A, B), merge_strategies(σ, τ)) ≈ compose(interpret(A, σ), interpret(B, τ)), and likewise for otimes.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalBayesianNetworks.left_leg","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalBayesianNetworks.left_leg-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"left_leg(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram)\nright_leg(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram)\n\nThe legs embedding the input and output foot into the apex (Catlab's left and right).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalBayesianNetworks.validate_composition","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalBayesianNetworks.validate_composition-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"validate_composition(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram) -> Nothing\n\nPost-hoc validation of a composite built without the typed-interface rule, such as the result of oapply with validate = false: the checks of validate. Rule 6 needs the components, so only the operations that receive them check it.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.best_response_dynamics","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.best_response_dynamics-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:OpenInfluenceDiagram}, AbstractDict, AbstractDict, Strategy}","category":"method","text":"best_response_dynamics(components, kernels, utilities, σ::Strategy; prior = nothing,\n                       continuation = nothing, no_forgetting = true, tol = 1e-9,\n                       max_rounds = 100, max_policies = 1_000_000, max_states,\n                       atol) -> NamedTuple\n\nStarting from the strategy σ of the whole chain, visit the components in order, replacing a component's part of the strategy by a best response in its current context (contextual_optimize) whenever that raises the component's value by more than tol, and repeat until a full round changes nothing. The result is an equilibrium (is_equilibrium), and the value of the whole chain never decreases, so the procedure ends; max_rounds bounds the rounds (converged = false when it is reached).\n\nIt returns (strategy, value, rounds, converged, values, changed): the final strategy, the chain's value under it (contextual_value of the composite in the chain's context), the number of rounds, whether the last round changed nothing, the chain's value before the first and after every change, and the index of the component changed at each change.\n\nThis is single policy updating at the level of components (Lauritzen and Nilsson's local optimisation for limited-memory influence diagrams). It finds an equilibrium, which can be worse than the optimum; which one depends on σ and on the order of the components. To find the optimum, use optimize on the closed composite.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.boundary_law","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.boundary_law-Tuple{RewardKernel}","category":"method","text":"boundary_law(r::RewardKernel; prior = nothing, atol = DEFAULT_ATOL) -> Vector{Float64}\nboundary_law(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram, kernels, utilities, σ::Strategy; prior, kwargs...)\nboundary_law(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram, m::InfluenceDiagramModel, σ = strategy(m); kwargs...)\n\nThe law ν_σ = μ K_f that a prefix f : X → Y under the fixed strategy σ induces on its joint output states, in joint_states order: ν_σ(y) = Σ_x μ(x) K_f(y | x), with the prior μ given as in contextual_value (left out when f has no inputs). It is the joint law of the hidden state and the remembered information at the boundary, not a product of marginals. It is the prior to pass to contextual_value or contextual_optimize for the suffix g of f ⋅ g: EU_{f;g}(σ, τ; μ, c) = L_σ + EU_g(τ; ν_σ, c), where L_σ = contextual_value(f; prior = μ, continuation = 0) is the reward the prefix earns.\n\nThe diagram forms interpret o first, with interpret's arguments and keywords.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.component_contexts","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.component_contexts-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:OpenInfluenceDiagram}, AbstractDict, AbstractDict, Strategy}","category":"method","text":"component_contexts(components, kernels, utilities, σ::Strategy; prior = nothing,\n                   continuation = nothing, no_forgetting = true, max_states,\n                   atol) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nThe context (prior = μᵢ, continuation = cᵢ) of each component of the chain components[1] ⋅ components[2] ⋅ … ⋅ components[n] under the strategy σ of the whole chain (ADR 0020). prior and continuation are the chain's own context, given as for contextual_value on the composite; they may be left out for an empty interface.\n\nThe contexts are the lens passes: μ₁ is the chain's prior and μᵢ₊₁ the boundary_law of component i from μᵢ; cₙ is the chain's continuation and cᵢ₋₁ the continuation_value of component i from cᵢ. Each μᵢ is a vector over the joint input states of component i and each cᵢ over its joint output states, in joint_states order; an empty interface has one joint state. By the contextual identity, contextual_value(components[i], …, σ; prior = μᵢ, continuation = cᵢ) differs from the value of the whole chain by the reward of the components before i, which i's strategy does not change.\n\nThe chain must compose (each component's outputs match the next one's inputs, as for compose, which checks rule 6 unless no_forgetting = false, the option for a limited-memory chain) and each decision name must belong to one component (ArgumentError otherwise). σ is restricted to each component's decisions and interpreted with interpret, whose keywords and errors apply.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.contextual_optimize","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.contextual_optimize-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram, AbstractDict, AbstractDict}","category":"method","text":"contextual_optimize(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram, kernels, utilities; prior = nothing,\n                    continuation = nothing, max_policies = 1_000_000,\n                    brute_force = false, max_states, atol) -> DecisionSolution\ncontextual_optimize(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram, m::InfluenceDiagramModel; kwargs...)\n\nMaximise contextual_value over the deterministic strategies of the decisions of the open component o : X → Y, in the explicit context of a prior μ on its joint input states and a continuation c on its joint output states, given as for contextual_value (ADR 0018, decision 4). The result is InfluenceDiagrams' DecisionSolution: the best value EU_o(σ*; μ, c), a best strategy σ* with a DeterministicPolicy for every decision name of o, and diagnostics that include nstrategies and method.\n\nOnly legal strategies are searched: each policy is a function of its decision's declared information set, which by rule 3 holds internal and observed input variables only. A hidden input is never read, so no strategy can maximise separately at each hidden boundary state; the bundle shows that doing so changes the problem (INF-HIDDEN-OBSTRUCTION).\n\nBy default the search is InfluenceDiagrams' own: the apex of o is bound to the tables of kernels and utilities as an InfluenceDiagramModel whose input variables keep no mechanism (they are its exogenous variables), the context becomes a DecisionContext with μ as the prior over the input variables and c as the terminal utility over the output variables (a repeated output repeats its name and is read on the diagonal; a pass-through variable is both), and optimize(m, ExhaustivePolicySearch(; max_policies); context, max_states, atol) solves it. The result and its diagnostics are that function's ((nstrategies, method = :value_table), with exact_fallback = true when its value table falls back to scoring strategy by strategy). It enumerates the strategies in the order of all_deterministic_strategies(apex(o)) (all_deterministic_policies for each decision, in decision_order, the first decision fastest) and returns the first one attaining the maximum. More than max_policies strategies raise PolicySearchTooLargeError. Decision variable elimination is never used, since it takes no context.\n\nWith brute_force = true every strategy, in the same order, is scored by contextual_value through interpret instead, an independent path that is slower and is the oracle the test suite compares with; its diagnostics are (nstrategies, method = :brute_force).\n\nThe context is what makes the result meaningful. With continuation the continuation_value of a suffix g under τ, the optimum is the best prefix strategy against that suffix; maximising that over τ gives the optimum of o ⋅ g, and so does maximising L_σ + contextual_optimize(g; prior = boundary_law(o, σ)) over σ. A component optimised against a context other than the one its neighbours induce (a zero continuation, say) can choose a strategy that composes badly: the bundle's Lean witness composes isolated optima to 1 where the composite attains 4 (INF-ISOLATED-OBSTRUCTION). There is no operation that optimises components one by one and composes them.\n\nArguments are checked as for interpret and contextual_value: o is validated, the prior and continuation are checked as there (a prior's entries nonnegative and summing to one within atol, as DecisionContext requires), at most max_states joint states of the apex are enumerated (ModelTooLargeError), and missing tables raise MissingKernelError or MissingUtilityError. The apex's variable, decision and utility names must be unique, since the search keys policies and tables by name: equally named decisions (a diagram tensored with itself, say) raise ArgumentError on both paths, while interpret and contextual_value still accept them. The model form reads kernels(m) and bound_utilities(m) and refuses evidence (ArgumentError).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.contextual_value","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.contextual_value-Tuple{RewardKernel}","category":"method","text":"contextual_value(r::RewardKernel; prior = nothing, continuation = nothing, atol = DEFAULT_ATOL) -> Float64\ncontextual_value(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram, kernels, utilities, σ::Strategy; prior, continuation, kwargs...)\ncontextual_value(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram, m::InfluenceDiagramModel, σ = strategy(m); kwargs...)\n\nThe expected utility EU_f(σ; μ, c) of an open component f : X → Y under the fixed strategy σ, in the context of a prior μ on its inputs and a continuation c on its outputs (ADR 0018, decision 4; the bundle's Score):\n\nEU_f(σ; μ, c) = Σ_x μ(x) Σ_y (W(y, x) + K(y | x) c(y)),\n\nwhere (K, W) is the reward kernel of f under σ. The diagram forms compute it with interpret, whose arguments and keywords (max_states, atol) they take.\n\nprior is a real vector over the joint input states, in joint_states order of dom(r) (the first input varies fastest), with nonnegative entries summing to one within atol (|Σ μ - 1| <= atol, as InfluenceDiagrams' DecisionContext requires; it is never renormalised), or a FiniteKernel state I → dom(r). It is the joint law of the hidden state and the remembered information at the boundary; no independence is assumed. It may be left out only when f has no inputs.\ncontinuation is a real (signed) vector over the joint output states, in joint_states order of codom(r): the value the rest of the problem earns from each boundary state. It may also be a RewardKernel codom(r) → I, whose reward row is the continuation (the continuation_value of a closed suffix). It may be left out only when f has no outputs. A zero continuation scores f's own reward only.\n\nA wrong length, a non-finite entry, a negative or unnormalised prior, or a context left out for a nonempty interface raises ArgumentError. On a closed composite, with both left out, the value is the expected utility.\n\nFor a serial composite the contextual identity holds (the bundle's INF-FIXED-CONTEXT, checked numerically by the test suite):\n\ncontextual_value(f ⋅ g; prior = μ, continuation = c)\n  == contextual_value(f; prior = μ, continuation = continuation_value(g; continuation = c))\n  == contextual_value(f; prior = μ, continuation = 0)\n     + contextual_value(g; prior = boundary_law(f; prior = μ), continuation = c)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.continuation_value","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.continuation_value-Tuple{RewardKernel}","category":"method","text":"continuation_value(r::RewardKernel; continuation = nothing) -> Vector{Float64}\ncontinuation_value(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram, kernels, utilities, σ::Strategy; continuation, kwargs...)\ncontinuation_value(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram, m::InfluenceDiagramModel, σ = strategy(m); kwargs...)\n\nThe value function c_τ of a suffix g : Y → Z under the fixed strategy τ, over the joint input states y of g in joint_states order:\n\nc_τ(y) = Σ_z (W_g(z, y) + K_g(z | y) c(z)),\n\nthe expected reward g earns from the boundary state y plus the continuation c (the argument continuation, as in contextual_value) of what follows g. When g is closed on the right (no outputs) continuation may be left out and c_τ(y) is the expected reward of g from y. It is the continuation to pass to contextual_value or contextual_optimize for the prefix f of f ⋅ g: EU_{f;g}(σ, τ; μ, c) = EU_f(σ; μ, c_τ).\n\nc_τ is the analyst's value function: it may depend on hidden boundary state, but the policies of τ still read only their declared information. The diagram forms interpret o first, with interpret's arguments and keywords.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.copy_reward_kernel","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.copy_reward_kernel-Tuple{FiniteSpace}","category":"method","text":"copy_reward_kernel(X::FiniteSpace) -> RewardKernel\n\nThe copy map X → X ⊗ X of FiniteKernels, with zero reward.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.discard_reward_kernel","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.discard_reward_kernel-Tuple{FiniteSpace}","category":"method","text":"discard_reward_kernel(X::FiniteSpace) -> RewardKernel\n\nThe discard map X → I of FiniteKernels, with zero reward. Discard is not natural in this category: f ; discard keeps the expected reward of f given each input, so it equals discard only when that reward is zero. Discarding an output does not discard the reward earned on the way to it.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.export_reward_certificate","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.export_reward_certificate-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram, AbstractDict, AbstractDict, Strategy}","category":"method","text":"export_reward_certificate(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram, kernels, utilities, σ::Strategy;\n                          numeric_mode = :binary64_exact, exact_tables = nothing,\n                          description = \"\", max_entries = 1_000_000)\n    -> OrderedDict{String,Any}\nexport_reward_certificate(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram, m::InfluenceDiagramModel,\n                          σ::Strategy = strategy(m); kwargs...)\n\nThe OpenInfluence.RewardCertificate/v1 record of the open influence diagram o under the deterministic strategy σ (ADR 0019): its structure, observability flags, chance and utility tables, the strategy, and the reward kernel (K, W) that interpret computes for them. The Lean checker in proofs/, lake exe check_certificate, reads it. The arguments are those of interpret, dictionary and model forms alike (a model with evidence raises ArgumentError).\n\nThe result is JSON-compatible data: an OrderedDict (OrderedCollections' type, the one BayesianNetworks and InfluenceDiagrams return for their documents) whose keys, and the fields of every named tuple inside it, are in the format's order, so JSON3.write emits the same bytes on every Julia version. Its keys are format, numeric_mode, metadata (producer, producer version, Julia version and description), network, observability, chance_tables, utilities, strategy and result:\n\nnetwork is the body of CategoricalBayesianNetworks' OpenNet.RawCertificate/v1 (open_network_certificate): dense 0-based variable indices in part order, ranks (the 0-based positions in a topological order of the causal-plus-information graph), state and input rows with their 1-based positions, and attributed boundary rows. The chance mechanisms come first, then each decision as a mechanism record whose reference is policy(name) and whose input rows are its information inputs.\nobservability holds one flag per boundary row.\nEvery table is a flat list of cells in lexicographic order of its axes, the last axis fastest, each axis over its variable's states in position order. Chance tables have the parents in input-position order, then the child; utilities their scope in position order; each distinct decision name has one policy, over its first decision's information inputs, whose cells are 1-based action state positions; K and W have the input rows, then the output rows. They are converted from the outputs-first, first-variable-fastest layout of the RewardKernel.\n\nWith numeric_mode = :binary64_exact a number cell is (f64 = word), the 16 lowercase hexadecimal digits of the cell's IEEE binary64 bit word, as in InfluenceDiagrams' export_dve_certificate. With numeric_mode = :rational_exact every chance and utility cell also carries an exact companion (q = (num, den), f64 = word), taken from exact_tables, a dictionary keyed by (:chance, mechanism_part_id, coordinates) and (:utility, utility_part_id, coordinates), with zero-based coordinate tuples in the table's axis order, to exact integers or rationals. It must cover every chance and utility cell and nothing else, and each companion must round, to nearest with ties to even, to its Float64 cell. The result cells stay binary64 words. No rational is inferred from a Float64, and no table is renormalised.\n\nErrors:\n\ninvalid keyword values (an unknown numeric_mode, exact_tables in binary64 mode or missing in rational mode, a malformed companion key or value, a description that is not a string, a negative max_entries) raise ArgumentError;\no is validated first and keeps the errors of validate; the tables and the strategy keep those of interpret (among them MissingKernelError, KernelBindingError, MissingUtilityError, UtilityScopeError, IncompleteStrategyError, PolicySignatureError, ModelTooLargeError, and ArgumentError for a StochasticPolicy);\nwhat the format cannot record raises RewardCertificateError with a code: :unset_reference, :unsupported_reference, :policy_reference_on_chance, :inconsistent_decision, :too_many_entries (more than max_entries cells in all, counted from the structure before any table is read or interpret runs), :non_finite, :missing_companion, :extra_companion and :companion_rounding.\n\nThe certificate records the diagram as Julia holds it, not the operations that built it; JSON parsing, this exporter, Catlab's colimits and Julia's Float64 arithmetic are trusted (ADR 0019). See the manual page \"Reward certificates\".\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.identity_reward_kernel","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.identity_reward_kernel-Tuple{FiniteSpace}","category":"method","text":"identity_reward_kernel(X::FiniteSpace) -> RewardKernel\n\nThe identity (id_X, 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.input_observability","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.input_observability-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"input_observability(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe OBSERVABILITY flag of each input variable of o, in interface order.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.is_equilibrium","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.is_equilibrium-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:OpenInfluenceDiagram}, AbstractDict, AbstractDict, Strategy}","category":"method","text":"is_equilibrium(components, kernels, utilities, σ::Strategy; tol = 1e-9, kwargs...) -> Bool\n\nWhether σ is an equilibrium of the chain (ADR 0020): every component's part of σ is a best response in its context, that is every one of regrets is at most tol. The other keywords are those of regrets.\n\nAn equilibrium need not be optimal: two components can each be best responding to the other while a joint change would do better. The global optimum of the closed composite, from optimize, is always an equilibrium.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.merge_strategies","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.merge_strategies-Tuple{Vararg{Strategy}}","category":"method","text":"merge_strategies(σs::Strategy...) -> Strategy\n\nThe strategy of a composite: the disjoint union of the components' strategies (ADR 0018, decision 3). A decision name with a policy in two of them raises DuplicatePolicyError, even when the two policies are equal: the components of a composite built by compose or glue have distinct decision names, and a strategy names each decision once.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.observability","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.observability-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagramOb}","category":"method","text":"observability(X::OpenInfluenceDiagramOb) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe OBSERVABILITY flags of the interface X, in interface order.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.observed_inputs","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.observed_inputs-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"observed_inputs(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nNames of the input variables of o that are :observed, in interface order: the inputs a decision of o may read.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.observed_outputs","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.observed_outputs-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"observed_outputs(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nNames of the output variables of o that are :observed, in interface order: the outputs a decision downstream of o may read.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.output_observability","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.output_observability-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"output_observability(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe OBSERVABILITY flag of each output variable of o, in interface order.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.regrets","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.regrets-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:OpenInfluenceDiagram}, AbstractDict, AbstractDict, Strategy}","category":"method","text":"regrets(components, kernels, utilities, σ::Strategy; prior = nothing,\n        continuation = nothing, no_forgetting = true, max_policies = 1_000_000,\n        max_states, atol) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nFor each component of the chain, how much more it could earn in its context (component_contexts) by changing its own part of σ alone: the optimum of contextual_optimize in that context minus the component's contextual_value there. Each regret is the gain in the value of the whole chain from that unilateral change, by the contextual identity, and is zero (up to rounding) for every component exactly when σ is an equilibrium. A component without decisions has regret zero.\n\nThe search for each component is contextual_optimize's, with its max_policies limit and its PolicySearchTooLargeError; the other keywords and errors are those of component_contexts.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.reward","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.reward-Tuple{RewardKernel}","category":"method","text":"reward(r::RewardKernel) -> Array{Float64}\n\nThe reward part W of r, laid out as the table of kernel(r) (output axes first, then input axes).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.reward_matrix","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.reward_matrix-Tuple{RewardKernel}","category":"method","text":"reward_matrix(r::RewardKernel) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nThe reward table of r as a length(codom) × length(dom) matrix, with rows and columns ordered as kernel_matrix(kernel(r)). Its column sums are the expected rewards given each input state.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.structured_cospan","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.structured_cospan-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"structured_cospan(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram) -> InfluenceDiagramCospan\n\nThe Catlab structured cospan of o, without its observability flags.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.swap_reward_kernel","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.swap_reward_kernel-Tuple{FiniteSpace, FiniteSpace}","category":"method","text":"swap_reward_kernel(X::FiniteSpace, Y::FiniteSpace) -> RewardKernel\n\nThe symmetry X ⊗ Y → Y ⊗ X of FiniteKernels, with zero reward.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"Catlab.CategoricalAlgebra.Cats.FreeDiagrams.Multispans.apex","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Catlab.CategoricalAlgebra.Cats.FreeDiagrams.Multispans.apex-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"apex(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram) -> InfluenceDiagram\n\nThe apex of o: the influence diagram whose variables the feet name.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"Catlab.CategoricalAlgebra.Cats.FreeDiagrams.Multispans.feet","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Catlab.CategoricalAlgebra.Cats.FreeDiagrams.Multispans.feet-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"feet(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram)\n\nThe two feet of o, input first, as VariableSpaces.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"Catlab.CategoricalAlgebra.Cats.FreeDiagrams.Multispans.legs","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Catlab.CategoricalAlgebra.Cats.FreeDiagrams.Multispans.legs-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"legs(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram)\n\nThe two legs of o, input first: Catlab ACSetTransformations from the (L-form) feet into the apex.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"Catlab.Theories.otimes","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Catlab.Theories.otimes-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram, OpenInfluenceDiagram}","category":"method","text":"otimes(A::OpenInfluenceDiagram, B::OpenInfluenceDiagram; no_forgetting = true) -> OpenInfluenceDiagram\nA ⊗ B\n\nPlace two open influence diagrams side by side: the apex is the coproduct (disjoint union) of the apexes, and the interfaces and their flags are concatenated, A's first. Names are kept, so tensoring a diagram with itself repeats them; the result is validated with unique_names = false. Rule 6 always holds here: no path orders a decision of A against one of B, so neither has anything to remember of the other.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"Catlab.Theories.otimes","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Catlab.Theories.otimes-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagramOb, OpenInfluenceDiagramOb}","category":"method","text":"otimes(X::OpenInfluenceDiagramOb, Y::OpenInfluenceDiagramOb) -> OpenInfluenceDiagramOb\nX ⊗ Y\n\nThe tensor of two interfaces: the variables of X followed by those of Y, with their flags.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"Catlab.Theories.otimes","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Catlab.Theories.otimes-Tuple{RewardKernel, RewardKernel}","category":"method","text":"otimes(a::RewardKernel, b::RewardKernel, cs::RewardKernel...) -> RewardKernel\na ⊗ b\n\nThe tensor (K₁ ⊗ K₂, W₁ ⊗ K₂ + K₁ ⊗ W₂) on dom(a) ⊗ dom(b) → codom(a) ⊗ codom(b), where ⊗ of kernels is FiniteKernels' tensor_kernel: the two run independently and their rewards add. Further reward kernels are folded on from the left.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"Catlab.WiringDiagrams.WiringDiagramAlgebras.oapply","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Catlab.WiringDiagrams.WiringDiagramAlgebras.oapply-Tuple{Catlab.WiringDiagrams.UndirectedWiringDiagrams.AbstractUWD, AbstractVector{<:OpenInfluenceDiagram}}","category":"method","text":"oapply(uwd::UndirectedWiringDiagram, diagrams::Vector{<:OpenInfluenceDiagram};\n       validate = true, no_forgetting = true) -> OpenInfluenceDiagram\noapply(uwd::UndirectedWiringDiagram, diagrams::Dict{Symbol,<:OpenInfluenceDiagram}; kw...)\n\nCompose open influence diagrams along an undirected wiring diagram (for example one written with @relation). Box i receives diagrams[i] (or, for the dictionary form, the diagram named like the box); its ports are the interface variables of the diagram, inputs first, then outputs. Diagrams whose ports meet at a junction must agree there in variable, states and reference (BayesianNetworks' InterfaceMismatchError, naming the junction) and in observability (ObservabilityMismatchError). An outer port on a junction that no box touches is refused with InterfaceMismatchError(:missing_junction, ...). A box count that differs from the number of diagrams raises ArgumentError.\n\nEach junction carries one flag, and each port the flag of its variable on its foot. A pass-through variable, one on both feet of a diagram, has two ports, an input port and an output port, whose flags differ when the diagram flags the variable differently on its two feet (see Open). Wired to the same junction, the two ports must agree like any two ports there, so different flags raise ObservabilityMismatchError. Wired to two junctions, each junction keeps its own flag; the colimit joins them into one variable, since both ports name it, and an outer port on either junction carries that junction's flag. Whether a decision may read the variable is then decided on the composite by rule 3, and rule 6 is checked as for compose.\n\nThe apex is the colimit computed by Catlab's oapply for structured multicospans. The inputs of the result are the outer-port variables without a generator (a mechanism or a decision) and its outputs the others, in outer-port order, each with its junction's flag. Undirected diagrams cannot express the typed-interface rule, so with validate = true each diagram is validated first and the composite afterwards, as for compose but without demanding unique names, rule 6 included unless no_forgetting = false. A junction that receives two generated variables gives BayesianNetworks' DuplicateGeneratorError or InfluenceDiagrams' DecisionUniquenessError, and a cycle CyclicBayesNetError or, through an information arc, InfluenceDiagrams' InvalidInformationSetError.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"InfluenceDiagrams.optimize","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#InfluenceDiagrams.optimize-Tuple{OpenInfluenceDiagram, AbstractDict, AbstractDict}","category":"method","text":"optimize(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram, kernels, utilities;\n         backend = DecisionVariableElimination(), atol = DEFAULT_ATOL, kwargs...) -> DecisionSolution\noptimize(o::OpenInfluenceDiagram, m::InfluenceDiagramModel; kwargs...)\n\nSolve a closed composite (ADR 0018, decision 4): o must have no inputs and no outputs, as compose(f, g) of an f : I → Y and a g : Y → I has. Its apex is instantiated with the tables of kernels and utilities (keyed as for interpret: by KernelRef or target variable name, and by utility name or utility_ref), and InfluenceDiagrams' optimize solves it with backend, a DecisionBackend (DecisionVariableElimination or ExhaustivePolicySearch). The result is that function's DecisionSolution: the maximal expected utility and a deterministic strategy for every decision of the composite. The keywords are passed on (atol, max_states).\n\nAn open o raises ArgumentError: a component with a boundary has no expected utility of its own until a prior and a continuation are given, which is what contextual_optimize takes. There is deliberately no operation that optimises the components of a composite one by one and composes the results; that is not sound (the bundle's INF-ISOLATED-OBSTRUCTION).\n\nThe model form reads kernels(m) and bound_utilities(m); a model with evidence raises ArgumentError (evidence composition is outside ADR 0018's first release). o is validated first. A missing table raises MissingKernelError or MissingUtilityError; the solver's own errors pass through, among them IrregularDiagramError from decision variable elimination on a composite without no-forgetting (built with no_forgetting = false; ExhaustivePolicySearch solves those) and PolicySearchTooLargeError.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"Reward certificates","page":"Reward certificates","location":"certificates/#Reward-certificates","category":"section","text":"export_reward_certificate writes an open influence diagram, a deterministic strategy for it and the reward kernel (K, W) that interpret computed for that strategy as an OpenInfluence.RewardCertificate/v1 record (ADR 0019). The Lean project in proofs/ checks such a record with lake exe check_certificate. The record ties the numbers Julia computed for one concrete diagram to the definition of the semantics the Lean theorems are stated for, cell by cell, with the error stated.\n\nusing CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams\n\nm = umbrella_model()\nchance = Open(influence_diagram(:Weather => [:sunny, :rainy],\n                                :Forecast => [:sunny, :cloudy, :rainy];\n                                mechanisms=[:Weather => (), :Forecast => :Weather],\n                                closed=false);\n              outputs=[:Weather, :Forecast], observed=[:Forecast])\ndecide = Open(influence_diagram(:Weather => [:sunny, :rainy],\n                                :Forecast => [:sunny, :cloudy, :rainy],\n                                :Umbrella => [:take, :leave];\n                                decisions=[:Umbrella => :Forecast],\n                                utilities=[:U => (:Weather, :Umbrella)], closed=false);\n              inputs=[:Weather, :Forecast], observed=[:Forecast])\nσ = Strategy(:Umbrella => deterministic_policy(m, :Umbrella, [:leave, :leave, :take]))\nkernels = Dict{Symbol,FiniteKernel}(x => kernel(m, x) for x in chance_names(syntax(m)))\n\ncertificate = export_reward_certificate(decide, kernels, bound_utilities(m), σ)\nclosed = export_reward_certificate(compose(chance, decide), kernels, bound_utilities(m), σ)\n\nThe arguments are those of interpret. The model form export_reward_certificate(o, m, σ = strategy(m)) reads kernels(m) and bound_utilities(m) through the apex's references, and refuses a model with evidence.\n\nThe result is JSON-compatible data, an OrderedDict{String,Any} whose keys, and the fields of every named tuple inside it, are in the format's order. JSON3, or another writer that keeps insertion order, therefore writes the same bytes on every Julia version:\n\nusing JSON3\nwrite(\"umbrella-decision.json\", JSON3.write(certificate) * \"\\n\")"},{"title":"What a certificate records","page":"Reward certificates","location":"certificates/#What-a-certificate-records","category":"section","text":"Key Content\nformat \"OpenInfluence.RewardCertificate/v1\"\nnumeric_mode \"binary64_exact\" or \"rational_exact\"\nmetadata producer, producer_version, julia_version and the caller's description\nnetwork the structure: variables, generators and boundary rows\nobservability one flag, \"observed\" or \"hidden\", per input row and per output row\nchance_tables one table per chance mechanism\nutilities one record per utility: its name, positioned scope rows and table\nstrategy one policy per distinct decision name\nresult {\"arithmetic\": \"binary64\", \"K\": [...], \"W\": [...]}\n\nThe JSON Schema (draft 2020-12) gives the shape of each field. Its property order is the key order of the certificate. Cross-field conditions are for the checker: cell counts, positions, ranks, flag counts, information locality, row sums and the result."},{"title":"The network","page":"Reward certificates","location":"certificates/#The-network","category":"section","text":"network has exactly the keys of the body of CategoricalBayesianNetworks' OpenNet.RawCertificate/v1 (open_network_certificate): variableCount, variableData, mechanisms, inputs and outputs, with the same record shapes and reference tags (noRef, named, pointMass and policy).\n\nVariable indices are dense and 0-based, in the apex's Variable part order. Positions (of states, inputs and scope) are the actual 1-based *_position attributes, and rows are written in part order with those positions, not sorted.\nEach variable's rank is its 0-based position in a topological order of the causal-plus-information graph, so ranks increase along every generator's input rows.\nThe generators are the chance mechanisms, in Mechanism part order, then the decisions, in Decision part order. A decision is a mechanism record whose name is its decision name, whose kernelRef is {\"tag\": \"policy\", \"decision\": name}, whose target is its action variable and whose inputRows are its information inputs, each with its information_position. Generator indices are 0-based positions in this list.\nBoundary rows hold the apex variable each foot variable maps to and the attributes read from the foot itself. A copied output is two rows on one variable, and a pass-through variable is a row on each foot.\n\nDecision precedence rows are not recorded, and neither is no-forgetting: the semantics for a fixed strategy reads neither."},{"title":"Cell layout","page":"Reward certificates","location":"certificates/#Cell-layout","category":"section","text":"Every table is a flat list of cells in lexicographic order of its axes, the last axis varying fastest, each axis running over its variable's states in position order. A table with no axes has one cell. The axes are:\n\na chance table: the parents in input-position order, then the child. A repeated parent is a repeated axis. The record is {\"mechanism\": g, \"cells\": [...]}, with g the generator index.\na utility: its scope rows in position order, a repeated variable being a repeated axis.\na policy: the information inputs, in position order, of the first decision generator with that name. Each cell is the 1-based state position of the chosen action. A ConstantPolicy is written as its table. Equally named decisions share one policy (ADR 0018), so they must agree in information axis lengths and action state count.\nK and W: the input rows, in row order, then the output rows, in row order.\n\nA RewardKernel holds its tables outputs first, with joint states enumerated first variable fastest (FiniteKernels' joint_states). The exporter converts them: the certificate cell at input state x and output state y is kernel(r).table[y..., x...] and reward(r)[y..., x...]. In the decision half of the umbrella above, the inputs are (Weather, Forecast) and there are no outputs, so W lists the expected utility for (sunny, sunny), (sunny, cloudy), (sunny, rainy), (rainy, sunny), and so on: 100, 100, 20, 0, 0, 70."},{"title":"Numeric modes","page":"Reward certificates","location":"certificates/#Numeric-modes","category":"section","text":"A number cell is an InfluenceDiagrams certificate value (as export_dve_certificate writes it), so the Lean side reuses that project's value decoder:\n\n{\"f64\": word}: word is 16 lowercase hexadecimal digits, the IEEE binary64 bit pattern. Its exact value is the dyadic rational the bits denote. Non-finite values are never written.\n{\"q\": {\"num\": \"...\", \"den\": \"...\"}, \"f64\": word}: an exact rational companion and the binary64 word, where f64 is the nearest-even rounding of q. The exact value of the cell is q.\n\nIn binary64_exact mode (the default) every cell is {\"f64\"}. A runtime acceptance of a kernel within atol does not make its binary64 rows sum to exactly one, and the checker makes no semantic claim when they do not. It reports the largest row deviation instead. Tables whose cells are dyadic (1/2, 1/4, 3/8, ...) are exact in binary64; the fixture dyadic.json is such a model.\n\nIn rational_exact mode every chance and utility cell is {\"q\", \"f64\"}, and the result cells stay {\"f64\"}. The companions come from exact_tables, a dictionary keyed like InfluenceDiagrams' companions, with zero-based coordinates in the table's axis order:\n\n(:chance, mechanism_part_id, Tuple(zero_based_coordinates)) => exact integer or rational\n(:utility, utility_part_id, Tuple(zero_based_coordinates))  => exact integer or rational\n\nIt must cover every chance and utility cell and nothing else. Each companion is checked to round, to nearest with ties to even, to the Float64 cell it accompanies, by BayesianNetworks' correctly rounded _nearest_binary64 (ADR 0016); a zero companion matches either signed zero. No rational is inferred from a Float64 and no table is renormalised. This mode is the way to certify a model such as the umbrella, whose decimal tables (0.7, 0.15, ...) are not dyadic."},{"title":"Limits and errors","page":"Reward certificates","location":"certificates/#Limits-and-errors","category":"section","text":"max_entries (default 1_000_000) bounds the total number of cells: the chance tables, utilities, policies, K and W. It is checked from the structure alone, before any table is read and before interpret runs.\n\nInvalid keyword values raise ArgumentError: an unknown numeric_mode, exact_tables in binary64_exact mode or missing in rational_exact mode, a companion key that is not of the form above (or two keys naming one cell) or a companion that is not an exact integer or rational, a description that is not a string, and a max_entries that is not a nonnegative integer.\n\nThe diagram is validated first, and keeps the errors of validate. The tables and the strategy keep those of interpret: MissingKernelError, KernelBindingError, MissingUtilityError, UtilityScopeError, IncompleteStrategyError, PolicySignatureError, ModelTooLargeError, and ArgumentError for a StochasticPolicy.\n\nWhat the format cannot record raises RewardCertificateError, whose code, path and message say what and where:\n\nCode Condition\n:unset_reference a space_ref or kernel_ref has no value\n:unsupported_reference a reference of a type the format has no tag for\n:policy_reference_on_chance a chance mechanism carries a PolicyRef\n:inconsistent_decision equally named decisions of different shapes\n:too_many_entries more than max_entries cells\n:non_finite a cell that is not finite\n:missing_companion a chance or utility cell without an exact companion\n:extra_companion a companion that matches no cell\n:companion_rounding a companion that does not round to its cell"},{"title":"Fixtures","page":"Reward certificates","location":"certificates/#Fixtures","category":"section","text":"scripts/write_certificate_fixtures.jl writes the fixtures the Lean checker reads into proofs/certificate-fixtures/, as compact JSON with a trailing newline:\n\nFile Content\numbrella-decision.json the decision half of the umbrella split, with Forecast observed on input\numbrella-closed.json the umbrella split composed back: empty interfaces, K = [1], W = [EU]\ntwo-stage-composite.json the oil wildcatter composed from its test and drill pieces\nrelay.json a pass-through variable and a copied output\ndyadic.json dyadic chance tables, so that binary64 rows sum to exactly one\numbrella-rational.json the closed umbrella in rational_exact mode\n\nThe test suite builds them with the same code and compares them with the committed files byte for byte, apart from metadata.julia_version, which names the Julia that ran the exporter. Run julia --project scripts/write_certificate_fixtures.jl --check to check them, and without --check to rewrite them after a deliberate change to the format, which has to be made in the Lean checker too."},{"title":"Trust boundary","page":"Reward certificates","location":"certificates/#Trust-boundary","category":"section","text":"A checker that accepts a certificate has checked the record, not the program that produced it (ADR 0019). These steps are trusted, not proved:\n\nJSON parsing;\nthis exporter;\nCatlab's colimits, which built the composite;\nJulia's Float64 arithmetic in interpret.\n\nA certificate certifies the diagram it records, not the operations that built it. A certificate of a composite shows that Julia's numbers match the Lean semantics for the composite as recorded; it does not show that Julia's composite is the Lean composite of its parts. Contextual values and contextual optimisation are not covered by version 1."},{"title":"Why optimisation does not compose","page":"Why optimisation does not compose","location":"tutorials/03_why_optimisation_does_not_compose/#Why-optimisation-does-not-compose","category":"section","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nSetup\nThe counterexample\nOptimising each component in isolation\nWhy it fails\nThe contextual repair\nPointwise maximisation at the boundary is not a strategy\nSummary\nReferences"},{"title":"Overview","page":"Why optimisation does not compose","location":"tutorials/03_why_optimisation_does_not_compose/#Overview","category":"section","text":"For a fixed strategy, the semantics of open influence diagrams is compositional: the reward kernel of a composite is the composite of the reward kernels (the first vignette, Open influence diagrams and composition). It is tempting to conclude that optimisation is compositional too: optimise each component on its own, then compose the optimal strategies. It is not. A study of serial influence semantics, carried out in three proof assistants before this package was written, refutes it with counterexamples (its claim INF-ISOLATED-OBSTRUCTION). This vignette builds one of them as two open influence diagrams, shows why it fails, repairs it with contextual optimisation, and shows why the obvious alternative repair, maximising separately at each boundary state, is not a strategy at all (the study’s INF-HIDDEN-OBSTRUCTION).\n\nThe phenomenon is familiar from limited-memory influence diagrams, where improving one policy at a time against the others reaches a local optimum that need not be global (Lauritzen and Nilsson 2001). What the open setting adds is a precise account of the context a component must be optimised against, and of what crosses the boundary."},{"title":"Setup","page":"Why optimisation does not compose","location":"tutorials/03_why_optimisation_does_not_compose/#Setup","category":"section","text":"using CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams"},{"title":"The counterexample","page":"Why optimisation does not compose","location":"tutorials/03_why_optimisation_does_not_compose/#The-counterexample","category":"section","text":"There are two components and three binary variables, with labels no and yes.\n\nThe early component generates a fair hidden bit H and holds an action A that observes nothing. Taking the action (A = yes) costs\n\nThe late component holds an action B that observes A only. It earns 4 when the early action was taken, and 2 when B matches the hidden bit.\n\nThe boundary carries H hidden and A observed: the late decision may read what the early decision did, but not the bit.\n\nbool = [:no, :yes]\nearly = Open(influence_diagram(:H => bool, :A => bool;\n                               mechanisms = [:H => ()],\n                               decisions = [:A => Symbol[]],\n                               utilities = [:EarlyCost => :A], closed = false);\n             outputs = [:H, :A], observed = [:A])\nlate = Open(influence_diagram(:H => bool, :A => bool, :B => bool;\n                              decisions = [:B => :A],\n                              utilities = [:LateBenefit => :A, :HiddenMatch => (:H, :B)],\n                              closed = false);\n            inputs = [:H, :A], observed = [:A])\nprintln(early)\nprintln(late)\n\nOpenInfluenceDiagram(2 variables, 1 mechanisms, 1 decisions, 1 utilities; Symbol[] -> [:H, :A!])\nOpenInfluenceDiagram(3 variables, 0 mechanisms, 1 decisions, 2 utilities; [:H, :A!] -> Symbol[])\n\nax(x) = FiniteAxis(x, bool)\nkernels = Dict{Symbol,FiniteKernel}(:H => state(FiniteSpace(:H, bool), [0.5, 0.5]))\nutilities = Dict{Symbol,AbstractUtility}(:EarlyCost => TabularUtility([ax(:A)], [0, -1]),\n                                         :LateBenefit => TabularUtility([ax(:A)], [0, 4]),\n                                         :HiddenMatch => TabularUtility([ax(:H), ax(:B)],\n                                                                        [2 0; 0 2]))\nnothing # hide\n\nThe composite is closed. Whatever the late policy, it cannot know the bit, so it matches it half the time and earns 1 from HiddenMatch on average. The early action therefore decides everything: not taking it is worth 1, taking it is worth -1 + 4 + 1 = 4. The table lists every strategy of the composite: the early action, and the late action for each value of A.\n\nwhole = compose(early, late)\nconstant(d, a) = Strategy(d => ConstantPolicy(a))\nlate_strategies = all_deterministic_strategies(apex(late))\n[(A = a, B = Tuple(policy_table(τ[:B])),\n  value = contextual_value(whole, kernels, utilities,\n                           merge_strategies(constant(:A, a), τ)))\n for a in bool for τ in late_strategies]\n\n8-element Vector{@NamedTuple{A::Symbol, B::Tuple{Symbol, Symbol}, value::Float64}}:\n (A = :no, B = (:no, :no), value = 1.0)\n (A = :no, B = (:yes, :no), value = 1.0)\n (A = :no, B = (:no, :yes), value = 1.0)\n (A = :no, B = (:yes, :yes), value = 1.0)\n (A = :yes, B = (:no, :no), value = 4.0)\n (A = :yes, B = (:yes, :no), value = 4.0)\n (A = :yes, B = (:no, :yes), value = 4.0)\n (A = :yes, B = (:yes, :yes), value = 4.0)\n\noptimize on the closed composite finds the optimum, 4, with the early action taken:\n\nbest = optimize(whole, kernels, utilities)\nbest.expected_utility, only(policy_table(best.strategy[:A]))\n\n(4.0, :yes)"},{"title":"Optimising each component in isolation","page":"Why optimisation does not compose","location":"tutorials/03_why_optimisation_does_not_compose/#Optimising-each-component-in-isolation","category":"section","text":"Optimising a component “in isolation” means optimising it against the context it actually receives, with nothing downstream: its true prior, and a zero continuation. The early component has no inputs, and its outputs (H, A) have four joint states. Against a zero continuation it sees only the cost of the action, so it does not take it:\n\niso_early = contextual_optimize(early, kernels, utilities; continuation = zeros(4))\niso_early.expected_utility, only(policy_table(iso_early.strategy[:A]))\n\n(0.0, :no)\n\nThe late component, given the boundary law that this choice induces on (H, A) (A = no for certain, H fair), finds every legal policy worth the same 1, and the search returns the first:\n\nν = boundary_law(early, kernels, utilities, iso_early.strategy)\niso_late = contextual_optimize(late, kernels, utilities; prior = ν)\n(boundary_law = ν,\n values = [Tuple(policy_table(τ[:B])) =>\n           contextual_value(late, kernels, utilities, τ; prior = ν)\n           for τ in late_strategies],\n chosen = Tuple(policy_table(iso_late.strategy[:B])))\n\n(boundary_law = [0.5, 0.5, 0.0, 0.0], values = [(:no, :no) => 1.0, (:yes, :no) => 1.0, (:no, :yes) => 1.0, (:yes, :yes) => 1.0], chosen = (:no, :no))\n\nEach component is optimal in its own context. Composed, they are worth 1, where the composite attains 4:\n\nisolated = contextual_value(whole, kernels, utilities,\n                            merge_strategies(iso_early.strategy, iso_late.strategy))\nisolated, best.expected_utility, isolated < best.expected_utility\n\n(1.0, 4.0, true)"},{"title":"Why it fails","page":"Why optimisation does not compose","location":"tutorials/03_why_optimisation_does_not_compose/#Why-it-fails","category":"section","text":"Two things go wrong, and both are about the boundary.\n\nThe prefix’s choice changes the suffix’s value. The late component’s value from a boundary state is not fixed: it is 4 more when the early action was taken. That value is the continuation of the early component, its value function over the boundary states. For the late policy that ignores A and plays no, continuation_value gives it over the joint states of (H, A), H varying fastest:\n\nτ = first(late_strategies)\nc = continuation_value(late, kernels, utilities, τ)\n[s => v for (s, v) in zip(joint_states(dom(interpret(late, kernels, utilities, τ))), c)]\n\n4-element Vector{Pair{Tuple{Symbol, Symbol}, Float64}}:\n   (:no, :no) => 2.0\n  (:yes, :no) => 0.0\n  (:no, :yes) => 6.0\n (:yes, :yes) => 4.0\n\nThe early component optimised against a zero continuation never sees the 4: it compares a cost of 1 against nothing. Isolation does not merely lose some precision; it optimises a different objective.\n\nThe hidden boundary state cannot be read. The late component would like to match H, which would earn 2 instead of 1. But H is hidden at the boundary, so a legal late policy is a function of A alone. Against the law of the isolated early choice no legal policy does better than 1, and nothing the late component can do signals to the early component that the action is worth taking."},{"title":"The contextual repair","page":"Why optimisation does not compose","location":"tutorials/03_why_optimisation_does_not_compose/#The-contextual-repair","category":"section","text":"The value of a composite is the value of either component in the context the other induces. For a prefix f with strategy σ, a suffix g with strategy τ, a prior μ and a continuation c, EU_{f;g}(σ, τ; μ, c) = EU_f(σ; μ, c_τ) = L_σ + EU_g(τ; ν_σ, c), where c_τ is the suffix’s value function, ν_σ the boundary law the prefix induces and L_σ the reward the prefix earns on its own (the study’s INF-FIXED-CONTEXT). Here μ and c are trivial, because the composite is closed.\n\nSo optimise the early component against the late component’s value function instead of against zero. It now takes the action, and attains the optimum:\n\nrepaired = contextual_optimize(early, kernels, utilities; continuation = c)\nrepaired.expected_utility, only(policy_table(repaired.strategy[:A]))\n\n(4.0, :yes)\n\nThe continuation depends on the suffix’s strategy, so the full search runs over the suffix’s strategies, optimising the prefix against each value function and keeping the best:\n\nsuffix_first = maximum(contextual_optimize(early, kernels, utilities;\n                                           continuation = continuation_value(late, kernels,\n                                                                             utilities, τ)).expected_utility\n                       for τ in late_strategies)\n\n4.0\n\nThe other form of the identity runs the other way: for each strategy of the prefix, add its own reward to the optimum of the suffix against the boundary law it induces. That reaches the optimum too:\n\nprefix_first = maximum(begin\n                           σ = constant(:A, a)\n                           L = contextual_value(early, kernels, utilities, σ;\n                                                continuation = zeros(4))\n                           ν_σ = boundary_law(early, kernels, utilities, σ)\n                           L + contextual_optimize(late, kernels, utilities;\n                                                   prior = ν_σ).expected_utility\n                       end\n                       for a in bool)\n\n4.0\n\nNeither form optimises a component on its own. Each optimises one component against a context that the other component’s strategy determines, and searches over that strategy. This is why the package provides contextual_value, contextual_optimize, continuation_value and boundary_law, and no operation that optimises the components of a composite one by one and composes the results."},{"title":"Pointwise maximisation at the boundary is not a strategy","page":"Why optimisation does not compose","location":"tutorials/03_why_optimisation_does_not_compose/#Pointwise-maximisation-at-the-boundary-is-not-a-strategy","category":"section","text":"A different repair suggests itself for the late component: from each boundary state, pick the action with the higher value. Against the boundary law of the isolated early choice that is worth 2, more than the legal optimum of 1:\n\ncs = [continuation_value(late, kernels, utilities, constant(:B, b)) for b in bool]\npointwise = sum(ν .* max.(cs...))\nlegal = contextual_optimize(late, kernels, utilities; prior = ν)\n(pointwise = pointwise, legal = legal.expected_utility)\n\n(pointwise = 2.0, legal = 1.0)\n\nBut the pointwise choice is not a policy of the late decision. At the boundary states (H = no, A = no) and (H = yes, A = no), which agree on A, it picks different actions:\n\nstates_HA = joint_states(dom(interpret(late, kernels, utilities, constant(:B, :no))))\n[s => bool[argmax([cs[1][j], cs[2][j]])] for (j, s) in enumerate(states_HA)]\n\n4-element Vector{Pair{Tuple{Symbol, Symbol}, Symbol}}:\n   (:no, :no) => :no\n  (:yes, :no) => :yes\n  (:no, :yes) => :no\n (:yes, :yes) => :yes\n\nIt reads the hidden bit. Written as a policy, the late decision would need H in its information set, and the typed-interface rule refuses a decision that reads a hidden input (rule 3):\n\npeeking = influence_diagram(:H => bool, :A => bool, :B => bool;\n                            decisions = [:B => (:H, :A)],\n                            utilities = [:LateBenefit => :A, :HiddenMatch => (:H, :B)],\n                            closed = false)\ntry\n    Open(peeking; inputs = [:H, :A], observed = [:A])\ncatch e\n    sprint(showerror, e)\nend\n\n\"DiagramInterfaceError: rule 3 (a decision reads only internal or observed input-foot variables) is violated: decision :B has the hidden input :H in its information set; declare it observed or remove the arc\"\n\nDeclaring H observed makes the policy legal, and the late component is then worth 2 against the same law. But that changes the problem: the early component hides H, and the two no longer compose.\n\npeek = Open(peeking; inputs = [:H, :A], observed = [:H, :A])\ncontextual_optimize(peek, kernels, utilities; prior = ν).expected_utility\n\n2.0\n\ntry\n    compose(early, peek)\ncatch e\n    sprint(showerror, e)\nend\n\n\"ObservabilityMismatchError: compose: outputs(A) vs inputs(B): variable :H at position 1 is :hidden on the left but :observed on the right; glued interface variables must agree on observability\""},{"title":"Summary","page":"Why optimisation does not compose","location":"tutorials/03_why_optimisation_does_not_compose/#Summary","category":"section","text":"In the counterexample each component, optimised against the context it receives with nothing downstream, chooses well for itself, and the composite of those choices is worth 1 where the optimum is 4. The early decision cannot see the reward its action makes possible downstream, and the late decision cannot read the hidden state that would let it do better. Optimising the early component against the late component’s value function, or the late component against the boundary law of each early choice, recovers the optimum; maximising pointwise over hidden boundary states does not give a policy, and the typed-interface rule refuses the information arc it would need. Optimisation of open influence diagrams is therefore always contextual: optimize solves a closed composite, and contextual_optimize solves one component against an explicit prior and continuation."},{"title":"References","page":"Why optimisation does not compose","location":"tutorials/03_why_optimisation_does_not_compose/#References","category":"section","text":"<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-LauritzenNilsson2001\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nLauritzen, Steffen L., and Dennis Nilsson. 2001. “Representing and Solving Decision Problems with Limited Information.” Management Science 47 (9): 1235–51. https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.47.9.1235.9779.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"title":"Composing a management decision with a zoo network","page":"Composing a management decision with a zoo network","location":"tutorials/02_management_decision_with_zoo_network/#Composing-a-management-decision-with-a-zoo-network","category":"section","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nSetup\nOpening the zoo network at a boundary\nA management component and a valuation component\nObservability at the boundary\nComposing and optimising the closed composite\nOptimising the decision component in context\nSummary\nReferences"},{"title":"Overview","page":"Composing a management decision with a zoo network","location":"tutorials/02_management_decision_with_zoo_network/#Overview","category":"section","text":"Ecological Bayesian networks are often built first as descriptions of a system, and a management decision is added later (Marcot et al. 2006; Runge et al. 2011). The usual way to add it is to edit the network into one larger influence diagram. With open influence diagrams the network can stay as it was published: it is opened at a boundary, a separate component holds the decision, its cost and how it acts on the system, and the pieces are composed. The decision then sees exactly what the boundary declares observed, and nothing else.\n\nThis vignette does that with the reference habitat network of the model zoo of EcologicalBayesianNetworks.jl. The network models soil moisture from climate and irrigation, and the vegetation, habitat quality and occupancy of a species from soil moisture and grazing pressure. In the zoo the grazing pressure is a root with a fixed prior. Here a manager sets it instead, through a grazing decision made on the observed soil moisture. The vignette opens the network at the boundary, attaches the decision component, composes, optimises the closed composite, and then optimises the decision component on its own against the context the network induces on its boundary."},{"title":"Setup","page":"Composing a management decision with a zoo network","location":"tutorials/02_management_decision_with_zoo_network/#Setup","category":"section","text":"load_model builds a model of the zoo. The reference habitat network is built into the zoo under the MIT licence, so it needs no download, and so is the SPEC section 46 grazing diagram, from which the decision component borrows its numbers.\n\nusing CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams\nusing EcologicalBayesianNetworks\n\nzoo = [\"reference_habitat_bn\", \"reference_grazing_id\"]\n[(name, model_info(name).licence, model_info(name).redistribution) for name in zoo]\n\n2-element Vector{Tuple{String, String, String}}:\n (\"reference_habitat_bn\", \"MIT\", \"builtin\")\n (\"reference_grazing_id\", \"MIT\", \"builtin\")\n\nhabitat = load_model(\"reference_habitat_bn\")\ngrazing = load_model(\"reference_grazing_id\")\n[x => states(syntax(habitat), x) for x in variable_names(syntax(habitat))]\n\n7-element Vector{Pair{Symbol, Vector{Symbol}}}:\n         :Climate => [:dry, :normal, :wet]\n      :Irrigation => [:low, :high]\n    :SoilMoisture => [:low, :medium, :high]\n :GrazingPressure => [:low, :high]\n      :Vegetation => [:sparse, :moderate, :dense]\n  :HabitatQuality => [:poor, :good]\n       :Occupancy => [:absent, :present]\n\nto_graphviz(syntax(habitat))\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe kernels of the zoo network, keyed by variable name, are the tables every component below reads:\n\nzoo_kernels = Dict{Symbol,FiniteKernel}(x => kernel(habitat, x)\n                                        for x in variable_names(syntax(habitat)))\nzoo_kernels[:GrazingPressure]\n\nFiniteKernel{Float64}(I → GrazingPressure{low,high})\n         ()\n  low   0.5\n  high  0.5"},{"title":"Opening the zoo network at a boundary","page":"Composing a management decision with a zoo network","location":"tutorials/02_management_decision_with_zoo_network/#Opening-the-zoo-network-at-a-boundary","category":"section","text":"Cut the network at soil moisture and grazing pressure, the two parents of vegetation. Upstream of the cut is the abiotic part, which generates soil moisture; downstream is the biotic part, which reads soil moisture and grazing pressure and generates vegetation, habitat quality and occupancy. Grazing pressure is generated by its own prior, a third piece. In the biotic part both cut variables are exogenous (closed = false), so they can be inputs.\n\nabiotic = bayesnet(:Climate => [:dry, :normal, :wet], :Irrigation => [:low, :high],\n                   :SoilMoisture => [:low, :medium, :high];\n                   mechanisms = [:SoilMoisture => (:Climate, :Irrigation)])\npressure_prior = bayesnet(:GrazingPressure => [:low, :high])\nbiotic = bayesnet(:SoilMoisture => [:low, :medium, :high], :GrazingPressure => [:low, :high],\n                  :Vegetation => [:sparse, :moderate, :dense],\n                  :HabitatQuality => [:poor, :good], :Occupancy => [:absent, :present];\n                  mechanisms = [:Vegetation => (:SoilMoisture, :GrazingPressure),\n                                :HabitatQuality => :Vegetation,\n                                :Occupancy => :HabitatQuality],\n                  closed = false)\nnothing # hide\n\nAs open influence diagrams the three pieces have no decisions and no utilities. Placing the abiotic part beside the prior (⊗) and composing with the biotic part (⋅) glues the network back together, and the result is the zoo network again:\n\nE0 = Open(InfluenceDiagram(abiotic); outputs = [:SoilMoisture])\nP0 = Open(InfluenceDiagram(pressure_prior); outputs = [:GrazingPressure])\nH0 = Open(InfluenceDiagram(biotic); inputs = [:SoilMoisture, :GrazingPressure],\n          outputs = [:Occupancy])\nN = (E0 ⊗ P0) ⋅ H0\nprintln(N)\ncanonicalize(apex(OpenBayesNet(N))) == canonicalize(reference_habitat_bn())\n\nOpenInfluenceDiagram(7 variables, 7 mechanisms, 0 decisions, 0 utilities; Symbol[] -> [:Occupancy])\n\ntrue\n\nThe semantics agrees too. Under the empty strategy the reward kernel of N has zero reward, and its probability part is the zoo model’s marginal of occupancy:\n\nrN = interpret(N, zoo_kernels, Dict{Symbol,AbstractUtility}(), Strategy())\nkernel(rN) ≈ marginal(habitat, :Occupancy), all(iszero, reward(rN))\n\n(true, true)"},{"title":"A management component and a valuation component","page":"Composing a management decision with a zoo network","location":"tutorials/02_management_decision_with_zoo_network/#A-management-component-and-a-valuation-component","category":"section","text":"The manager replaces the prior on grazing pressure. The management component holds a decision, GrazingManagement (exclude, reduce or maintain grazing), made on the soil moisture; a mechanism for the grazing pressure that the decision achieves; and the cost of the decision. It takes the soil moisture as an observed input and passes it on, with the grazing pressure, to the biotic part. What the manager observed is information for the decision, and the biotic part has no decision to inform, so the soil moisture leaves the component hidden: observed takes a named tuple that flags each foot separately, and here names the soil moisture on the input foot only. The kernel of the grazing pressure and the cost are those of the zoo’s SPEC section 46 grazing diagram: excluding grazing makes low pressure likely but not certain, and costs 40, reducing it costs 15, maintaining it nothing.\n\nmanagement = influence_diagram(:SoilMoisture => [:low, :medium, :high],\n                               :GrazingManagement => [:exclude, :reduce, :maintain],\n                               :GrazingPressure => [:low, :high];\n                               mechanisms = [:GrazingPressure => :GrazingManagement],\n                               decisions = [:GrazingManagement => :SoilMoisture],\n                               utilities = [:ManagementCost => :GrazingManagement],\n                               closed = false)\nM = Open(management; inputs = [:SoilMoisture],\n         outputs = [:SoilMoisture, :GrazingPressure],\n         observed = (inputs = [:SoilMoisture],))\nprintln(M)\nutility_table(bound_utilities(grazing)[:ManagementCost])\n\nOpenInfluenceDiagram(3 variables, 1 mechanisms, 1 decisions, 1 utilities; [:SoilMoisture!] -> [:SoilMoisture, :GrazingPressure])\n\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n -40.0\n -15.0\n   0.0\n\nA valuation component scores the outcome. The zoo network has no utility, so the value of the species’ presence is a choice made here: 1000 against a management cost of at most 40, a reserve that values the species highly.\n\nV = Open(influence_diagram(:Occupancy => [:absent, :present];\n                           utilities = [:ConservationBenefit => :Occupancy],\n                           closed = false);\n         inputs = [:Occupancy])\nbenefit(value) = TabularUtility([FiniteAxis(:Occupancy, [:absent, :present])],\n                                [0.0, value])\nutilities = Dict{Symbol,AbstractUtility}(:ManagementCost => bound_utilities(grazing)[:ManagementCost],\n                                         :ConservationBenefit => benefit(1000.0))\nprintln(V)\n\nOpenInfluenceDiagram(1 variables, 0 mechanisms, 0 decisions, 1 utilities; [:Occupancy] -> Symbol[])\n\nThe kernels of the composite are those of the zoo network, except that grazing pressure is now generated by the management decision:\n\nkernels = merge(zoo_kernels, Dict(:GrazingPressure => kernel(grazing, :GrazingPressure)))\nkernels[:GrazingPressure]\n\nFiniteKernel{Float64}(GrazingManagement{exclude,reduce,maintain} → GrazingPressure{low,high})\n        exclude  reduce  maintain\n  low      0.95     0.6      0.15\n  high     0.05     0.4      0.85"},{"title":"Observability at the boundary","page":"Composing a management decision with a zoo network","location":"tutorials/02_management_decision_with_zoo_network/#Observability-at-the-boundary","category":"section","text":"The decision reads the soil moisture across the boundary, so the boundary must declare it observed. The abiotic part as opened above declares nothing observed, as Open does by default, and so it does not match the management component:\n\ntry\n    compose(E0, M)\ncatch e\n    sprint(showerror, e)\nend\n\n\"ObservabilityMismatchError: compose: outputs(A) vs inputs(B): variable :SoilMoisture at position 1 is :hidden on the left but :observed on the right; glued interface variables must agree on observability\"\n\nNor may the management component hide the soil moisture from its own decision (rule 3 of the typed-interface rule):\n\ntry\n    Open(management; inputs = [:SoilMoisture], outputs = [:SoilMoisture, :GrazingPressure])\ncatch e\n    sprint(showerror, e)\nend\n\n\"DiagramInterfaceError: rule 3 (a decision reads only internal or observed input-foot variables) is violated: decision :GrazingManagement has the hidden input :SoilMoisture in its information set; declare it observed or remove the arc\"\n\nSo the abiotic part is opened with soil moisture observed. The management component passes the soil moisture on hidden, so the biotic part is used exactly as it was opened above, with everything hidden: it has no decision, and nothing downstream reads the soil moisture as information. Grazing pressure stays hidden too: the manager chooses the management, not the pressure it achieves. Both boundaries now match:\n\nE = Open(InfluenceDiagram(abiotic); outputs = [:SoilMoisture], observed = [:SoilMoisture])\nH = H0\nprintln(E)\ninterface_matches(codom(E), dom(M)), interface_matches(codom(M), dom(H))\n\nOpenInfluenceDiagram(3 variables, 3 mechanisms, 0 decisions, 0 utilities; Symbol[] -> [:SoilMoisture!])\n\n(true, true)"},{"title":"Composing and optimising the closed composite","page":"Composing a management decision with a zoo network","location":"tutorials/02_management_decision_with_zoo_network/#Composing-and-optimising-the-closed-composite","category":"section","text":"The four components compose in sequence into a closed influence diagram:\n\nwhole = E ⋅ M ⋅ H ⋅ V\nprintln(whole)\n\nOpenInfluenceDiagram(8 variables, 7 mechanisms, 1 decisions, 2 utilities; Symbol[] -> Symbol[])\n\nto_graphviz(apex(whole); states = false)\n\n(Image: )\n\nIts apex is an ordinary influence diagram, so optimize solves it with the solvers of InfluenceDiagrams.jl (decision variable elimination by default). The policy table is indexed by the soil moisture (low, medium, high):\n\nbest = optimize(whole, kernels, utilities)\nround(best.expected_utility; digits = 2), policy_table(best.strategy[:GrazingManagement])\n\n(469.82, [:reduce, :reduce, :exclude])\n\nThe optimal policy reacts to the soil moisture, and it is worth more than any fixed action:\n\nfixed = [a => round(contextual_value(whole, kernels, utilities,\n                                     Strategy(:GrazingManagement => ConstantPolicy(a)));\n                    digits = 2)\n         for a in (:exclude, :reduce, :maintain)]\n\n3-element Vector{Pair{Symbol, Float64}}:\n  :exclude => 468.12\n   :reduce => 468.32\n :maintain => 451.45\n\nWith a lower value on the species’ presence the decision is not worth taking. Valued at 100, the cost of management is never repaid and the optimal policy maintains grazing whatever the soil moisture:\n\nlow_value = merge(utilities, Dict(:ConservationBenefit => benefit(100.0)))\npolicy_table(optimize(whole, kernels, low_value).strategy[:GrazingManagement])\n\n3-element Vector{Symbol}:\n :maintain\n :maintain\n :maintain"},{"title":"Optimising the decision component in context","page":"Composing a management decision with a zoo network","location":"tutorials/02_management_decision_with_zoo_network/#Optimising-the-decision-component-in-context","category":"section","text":"The management component is open: it has inputs and outputs, so it has no expected utility of its own. It has one in a context: a law μ on its input and a value c for each of its output states. The rest of the composite supplies both.\n\nboundary_law of E is the law the abiotic part induces on the soil moisture.\ncontinuation_value of H ⋅ V is the expected conservation benefit that the biotic part and the valuation earn from each joint state of soil moisture and grazing pressure.\n\nν = boundary_law(E, kernels, utilities, Strategy())\nrHV = interpret(H ⋅ V, kernels, utilities, Strategy())\nc = continuation_value(rHV)\n(soil_moisture = round.(ν; digits = 3),\n continuation = [y => round(v; digits = 1)\n                 for (y, v) in zip(joint_states(dom(rHV)), c)])\n\n(soil_moisture = [0.288, 0.403, 0.309], continuation = [(:low, :low) => 395.2, (:medium, :low) => 521.8, (:high, :low) => 607.0, (:low, :high) => 338.9, (:medium, :high) => 451.6, (:high, :high) => 521.8])\n\ncontextual_optimize maximises the component’s value in that context over its legal strategies, the policies that read the soil moisture. It finds the optimum of the closed composite and the same policy:\n\nlocal_best = contextual_optimize(M, kernels, utilities; prior = ν, continuation = c)\n(local_best.expected_utility ≈ best.expected_utility,\n local_best.strategy == best.strategy, local_best.diagnostics)\n\n(true, true, (nstrategies = 27, method = :value_table))\n\nThis is the contextual identity: for every strategy σ of the management component, its value in the context (ν, c) is the expected utility of the whole composite. The abiotic part earns no reward of its own, so nothing is added for it.\n\nall(contextual_value(M, kernels, utilities, σ; prior = ν, continuation = c) ≈\n    contextual_value(whole, kernels, utilities, σ)\n    for σ in (best.strategy,\n              (Strategy(:GrazingManagement => ConstantPolicy(a))\n               for a in (:exclude, :reduce, :maintain))...))\n\ntrue\n\nThe context matters. Optimised against a zero continuation, as if nothing downstream depended on the grazing, the component sees only the cost of management and maintains grazing everywhere. Composed with the network, that choice is worth less than the optimum:\n\nisolated = contextual_optimize(M, kernels, utilities; prior = ν, continuation = zeros(6))\n(policy = policy_table(isolated.strategy[:GrazingManagement]),\n composed_value = round(contextual_value(whole, kernels, utilities, isolated.strategy);\n                        digits = 2),\n optimum = round(best.expected_utility; digits = 2))\n\n(policy = [:maintain, :maintain, :maintain], composed_value = 451.45, optimum = 469.82)\n\nHere the failure is easy to see, because the context that was left out is the whole benefit. The third vignette, Why optimisation does not compose, shows that it goes wrong even when each component is optimised against the context its neighbours actually induce, one component at a time."},{"title":"Summary","page":"Composing a management decision with a zoo network","location":"tutorials/02_management_decision_with_zoo_network/#Summary","category":"section","text":"The reference habitat network of the model zoo was opened at the boundary between soil moisture and grazing pressure and the rest of the network, and composed back to itself with the same kernel of occupancy. Replacing the prior on grazing pressure by a management component, with a decision on the observed soil moisture, the SPEC section 46 kernel of achieved grazing pressure and its cost, and adding a valuation of occupancy, gave a closed influence diagram that optimize solves. The boundary had to declare the soil moisture observed where the decision reads it, on the input foot of the management component: an embedded network hides everything, and a decision that reads a hidden input is refused. Nothing downstream needed it, so it left the component hidden, and the biotic part of the network was composed as it was opened. Optimised on its own against the law the network induces on its input and the value the network earns from its outputs, the decision component attains the optimum of the whole; optimised against a context that leaves the network out, it does not."},{"title":"References","page":"Composing a management decision with a zoo network","location":"tutorials/02_management_decision_with_zoo_network/#References","category":"section","text":"<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Marcot2006\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nMarcot, Bruce G., J. Douglas Steventon, Glenn D. Sutherland, and Robert K. McCann. 2006. “Guidelines for Developing and Updating Bayesian Belief Networks Applied to Ecological Modeling and Conservation.” Canadian Journal of Forest Research 36 (12): 3063–74. https://doi.org/10.1139/x06-135.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Runge2011\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nRunge, Michael C., Sarah J. Converse, and James E. Lyons. 2011. “Which Uncertainty? Using Expert Elicitation and Expected Value of Information to Design an Adaptive Program.” Biological Conservation 144 (4): 1214–23. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biocon.2010.12.020.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"title":"Lenses and equilibria","page":"Lenses and equilibria","location":"games/#Lenses-and-equilibria","category":"section","text":"Optimisation of open influence diagrams is contextual (ADR 0018): a component f : X → Y is valued against a prior μ on its inputs and a continuation c on its outputs, and the optimum of a composite is not the composite of the components' optima. ADR 0020 adds the solution concept that does compose, equilibrium in the sense of open games, and the lenses that carry the contexts it needs."},{"title":"The lens of a component","page":"Lenses and equilibria","location":"games/#The-lens-of-a-component","category":"section","text":"Under a fixed strategy a component is a reward kernel (K, W). It transports a context in two directions:\n\nforward, a prior μ on its inputs becomes the law ν = μ K on its outputs (boundary_law);\nbackward, a continuation c on its outputs becomes the value function x ↦ Σ_y (W(y, x) + K(y | x) c(y)) on its inputs (continuation_value).\n\nThe pair is a lens. For a chain f₁ ⋅ f₂ ⋅ … ⋅ fₙ under a strategy σ, the forward pass gives each component the law on its inputs and the backward pass the value of its outputs. component_contexts returns these contexts (μᵢ, cᵢ). By the contextual identity, component i's value in its context is the value of the whole chain less the reward of the components before it, which i's own strategy cannot change. The Lean module Lens proves that the lens of a serial composite is the composite of the lenses (lens_comp), so the contexts do not depend on how the chain is bracketed."},{"title":"Equilibria","page":"Lenses and equilibria","location":"games/#Equilibria","category":"section","text":"A strategy of the chain is an equilibrium when every component's part of it is a best response in that component's context: no component can raise the value of the whole chain by changing its own strategy alone. regrets returns, for each component, what it would gain by its best response (contextual_optimize in its context); is_equilibrium checks that every regret is at most a tolerance.\n\nchain = [early, late]                 # e.g. the components of the third vignette\nσ = merge_strategies(Strategy(:A => ConstantPolicy(:no)), Strategy(:B => ConstantPolicy(:no)))\ncomponent_contexts(chain, kernels, utilities, σ)   # [(prior, continuation), ...]\nregrets(chain, kernels, utilities, σ)              # [3.0, 0.0]: the early component gains 3\nis_equilibrium(chain, kernels, utilities, σ)       # false\n\nbest_response_dynamics replaces one component's strategy at a time by a best response in its current context, while that strictly improves it, and stops at an equilibrium. The value of the chain never decreases."},{"title":"What an equilibrium is not","page":"Lenses and equilibria","location":"games/#What-an-equilibrium-is-not","category":"section","text":"An equilibrium is a person-by-person optimum: each component is optimal given the others. The global optimum of the closed composite is always an equilibrium (seq_sound), but an equilibrium can be worth less. Two decisions that must agree, where the later does not observe the earlier, have an equilibrium in which neither acts, worth 1, and one in which both act, worth 2 (coordination_equilibrium_not_optimal). Best-response dynamics started from \"neither acts\" stays there. This is the local optimality of single policy updating in limited-memory influence diagrams (Lauritzen and Nilsson, 2001), at the level of components.\n\nSo:\n\nuse optimize on the closed composite for the optimum;\nuse component_contexts to see what each component is optimised against, and regrets to check whether a proposed strategy can be improved by any one component on its own;\nuse best_response_dynamics when components are owned or revised separately and the question is where separate revision settles, not what is best overall.\n\nIn the third vignette's counterexample, where components optimised in isolation compose to 1 against an optimum of 4, the equilibria are exactly the strategies that take the early action, and they attain 4: optimising each component against the context its neighbours induce repairs that example, though not every example.\n\nLimited-memory chains, where the later decisions do not remember the earlier ones, are composed with no_forgetting = false, a keyword all four functions accept and pass to compose. Behavioural strategies and the tensor of games are outside ADR 0020."},{"title":"Open influence diagrams and composition","page":"Open influence diagrams and composition","location":"tutorials/01_open_influence_diagrams/#Open-influence-diagrams-and-composition","category":"section","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nSetup\nSplitting the umbrella problem\nThe typed-interface rule\nComposing the umbrella problem back\nSemantics for a fixed strategy: reward kernels\nFunctoriality, checked numerically\nA first look at optimisation\nSummary\nReferences"},{"title":"Overview","page":"Open influence diagrams and composition","location":"tutorials/01_open_influence_diagrams/#Overview","category":"section","text":"An influence diagram adds decisions and utilities to a Bayesian network (Howard and Matheson 2005): a decision is a variable whose value is chosen by a policy that reads the decision’s information set, and the utilities score the outcome. An open influence diagram has an interface as well. Some variables are inputs, provided by the environment, and some are outputs, offered to it. As for open Bayesian networks (Fong 2012), the interface is a structured cospan (Baez and Courser 2020): the apex is the influence diagram, the two feet are VariableSpaces, and composition glues the outputs of one diagram to the inputs of the next by a pushout.\n\nA decision problem needs one more thing at the boundary. A later decision may read a variable that crosses the interface only if that variable is remembered information, not hidden state. So every interface variable carries an observability flag, :observed or :hidden, and two interfaces match only if their flags do. This vignette splits the umbrella problem into a chance component and a decision component, shows what the typed-interface rule refuses, composes the pieces back with compose, glue, otimes and oapply, and checks numerically that the semantics, a reward kernel for each fixed strategy, sends composition to composition."},{"title":"Setup","page":"Open influence diagrams and composition","location":"tutorials/01_open_influence_diagrams/#Setup","category":"section","text":"The umbrella problem has a weather variable, a forecast that depends on it, and a decision, whether to take the umbrella, made on the forecast. The utility U scores the weather and the decision.\n\nusing CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams\n\nm = umbrella_model()\numbrella = syntax(m)\n(variables = variable_names(umbrella), decisions = decision_names(umbrella),\n information = information_names(umbrella, :Umbrella), utilities = utility_names(umbrella))\n\n(variables = [:Weather, :Forecast, :Umbrella], decisions = [:Umbrella], information = [:Forecast], utilities = [:U])\n\nto_graphviz(umbrella; states = false)\n\n(Image: )"},{"title":"Splitting the umbrella problem","page":"Open influence diagrams and composition","location":"tutorials/01_open_influence_diagrams/#Splitting-the-umbrella-problem","category":"section","text":"Cut the diagram between the forecast and the decision. The chance component generates Weather and Forecast and offers both; the decision component takes both as inputs, holds the decision and the utility. In the decision component neither input has a mechanism (closed = false allows that).\n\nchance_diagram = influence_diagram(:Weather => [:sunny, :rainy],\n                                   :Forecast => [:sunny, :cloudy, :rainy];\n                                   mechanisms = [:Weather => (), :Forecast => :Weather],\n                                   closed = false)\ndecide_diagram = influence_diagram(:Weather => [:sunny, :rainy],\n                                   :Forecast => [:sunny, :cloudy, :rainy],\n                                   :Umbrella => [:take, :leave];\n                                   decisions = [:Umbrella => :Forecast],\n                                   utilities = [:U => (:Weather, :Umbrella)],\n                                   closed = false)\nnothing # hide\n\nOpen names the interface, and its keyword observed names the interface variables a decision may read. The weather crosses the boundary because the utility needs it, but no one sees it before deciding: it stays hidden. The forecast is observed.\n\nA = Open(chance_diagram; outputs = [:Weather, :Forecast], observed = [:Forecast])\nB = Open(decide_diagram; inputs = [:Weather, :Forecast], observed = [:Forecast])\nprintln(A)\nprintln(B)\n\nOpenInfluenceDiagram(2 variables, 2 mechanisms, 0 decisions, 0 utilities; Symbol[] -> [:Weather, :Forecast!])\nOpenInfluenceDiagram(3 variables, 0 mechanisms, 1 decisions, 1 utilities; [:Weather, :Forecast!] -> Symbol[])\n\nAn observed variable is printed with a !. The flags are part of the interface:\n\noutput_observability(A), input_observability(B), observed_inputs(B)\n\n([:hidden, :observed], [:hidden, :observed], [:Forecast])\n\ndom and codom return the interfaces as objects of the category, a VariableSpace with one flag per variable:\n\ncodom(A) == dom(B)\n\ntrue\n\nThe default is that everything is hidden. It is the conservative choice: an interface carries information only where it says so. An open Bayesian network embeds as an open influence diagram with no decisions and every interface variable hidden:\n\nbn_piece = Open(bayesnet(:Weather => [:sunny, :rainy],\n                         :Forecast => [:sunny, :cloudy, :rainy];\n                         mechanisms = [:Weather => (), :Forecast => :Weather]);\n                outputs = [:Weather, :Forecast])\nembedded = OpenInfluenceDiagram(bn_piece)\noutput_observability(embedded)\n\n2-element Vector{Symbol}:\n :hidden\n :hidden"},{"title":"The typed-interface rule","page":"Open influence diagrams and composition","location":"tutorials/01_open_influence_diagrams/#The-typed-interface-rule","category":"section","text":"The typed-interface rule of open Bayesian networks extends to decisions and utilities. Every variable has at most one generator, a chance mechanism or a decision; an input has no generator in the apex; a decision reads only internal variables or observed inputs; a utility may read any variable of the apex; the causal-plus-information graph is acyclic; and composition preserves no-forgetting. Open checks the rule and throws the first violation as a typed error.\n\nA decision that reads a hidden input violates rule 3. Declaring the forecast hidden leaves the umbrella decision reading a variable that, across the boundary, no one has seen:\n\ntry\n    Open(decide_diagram; inputs = [:Weather, :Forecast])\ncatch e\n    sprint(showerror, e)\nend\n\n\"DiagramInterfaceError: rule 3 (a decision reads only internal or observed input-foot variables) is violated: decision :Umbrella has the hidden input :Forecast in its information set; declare it observed or remove the arc\"\n\nAn input that has a mechanism violates rule 2: the weather would then be generated twice once the components are glued.\n\ntry\n    Open(chance_diagram; inputs = [:Weather], outputs = [:Forecast])\ncatch e\n    sprint(showerror, e)\nend\n\n\"DiagramInterfaceError: rule 2 (an input-foot variable has no generator in the apex) is violated: input :Weather is generated by the mechanism :Weather_mechanism\"\n\nA variable without a generator must be an input, as for an open network. Here the weather has no mechanism in the decision component and is left off the interface:\n\ntry\n    Open(decide_diagram; inputs = [:Forecast], observed = [:Forecast])\ncatch e\n    sprint(showerror, e)\nend\n\n\"InterfaceError: rule 3 (every mechanism-free apex variable is an input) is violated by variable(s) Weather (ids 1)\"\n\nThe flags are checked at composition as well. A chance component that hides the forecast does not match a decision component that expects to observe it, even though the names and states agree:\n\nA_hidden = Open(chance_diagram; outputs = [:Weather, :Forecast])\ntry\n    compose(A_hidden, B)\ncatch e\n    sprint(showerror, e)\nend\n\n\"ObservabilityMismatchError: compose: outputs(A) vs inputs(B): variable :Forecast at position 2 is :hidden on the left but :observed on the right; glued interface variables must agree on observability\"\n\nRule 6 concerns composites. In the oil wildcatter’s problem a test decision comes before a drilling decision. Split between them, each component has no-forgetting on its own, but a drilling decision that reads the test result and not whether a test was made forgets an earlier decision once the two are composed:\n\ntest_part = Open(influence_diagram(:Oil => [:dry, :wet], :Test => [:test, :skip],\n                                   :Result => [:pos, :neg, :none];\n                                   mechanisms = [:Oil => (), :Result => (:Oil, :Test)],\n                                   decisions = [:Test => Symbol[]],\n                                   utilities = [:TestCost => :Test], closed = false);\n                 outputs = [:Oil, :Test, :Result], observed = [:Test, :Result])\ndrill_diagram(information) =\n    influence_diagram(:Oil => [:dry, :wet], :Test => [:test, :skip],\n                      :Result => [:pos, :neg, :none], :Drill => [:drill, :dont];\n                      decisions = [:Drill => information],\n                      utilities = [:Payoff => (:Oil, :Drill)], closed = false)\nforgetful = Open(drill_diagram((:Result,)); inputs = [:Oil, :Test, :Result],\n                 observed = [:Test, :Result])\ntry\n    compose(test_part, forgetful)\ncatch e\n    sprint(showerror, e)\nend\n\n\"NoForgettingError: the components have no-forgetting but the composite does not: decision :Drill comes after decision :Test but does not know :Test (the information arc :Test -> :Drill is missing); pass :Test across the boundary as an observed variable and add it to the information set of :Drill, or compose with `no_forgetting = false`\"\n\nWith the test passed on as an observed variable and read by the drilling decision, the composite is the oil wildcatter’s diagram:\n\ndrill_part = Open(drill_diagram((:Test, :Result)); inputs = [:Oil, :Test, :Result],\n                  observed = [:Test, :Result])\nis_isomorphic(apex(compose(test_part, drill_part)), two_stage_diagram())\n\ntrue"},{"title":"Composing the umbrella problem back","page":"Open influence diagrams and composition","location":"tutorials/01_open_influence_diagrams/#Composing-the-umbrella-problem-back","category":"section","text":"compose (or ⋅) is strict sequential composition: the outputs of the first diagram must equal the inputs of the second, position by position, with the same states and the same flags. The composite is the pushout of the two apexes along the shared foot. Here it has no interface left, and it is the umbrella diagram again:\n\nC = compose(A, B)\nprintln(C)\nis_isomorphic(apex(C), umbrella_diagram()), A ⋅ B == C\n\nOpenInfluenceDiagram(3 variables, 2 mechanisms, 1 decisions, 1 utilities; Symbol[] -> Symbol[])\n\n(true, true)\n\nglue composes along explicit pairs of names, which may differ (the glued variable keeps its name from the first diagram), and leaves the rest of the interface open. Renaming the decision component’s forecast to Signal and gluing along both variables gives the umbrella diagram again; gluing only the forecast, with the decision component’s weather renamed Season, leaves the two weather variables on the interface:\n\nB_signal = Open(rename_variable(decide_diagram, :Forecast => :Signal);\n                inputs = [:Weather, :Signal], observed = [:Signal])\nG = glue(A, B_signal; along = [:Weather => :Weather, :Forecast => :Signal])\nB_season = Open(rename_variable(decide_diagram, :Weather => :Season);\n                inputs = [:Season, :Forecast], observed = [:Forecast])\nH = glue(A, B_season; along = [:Forecast => :Forecast])\nprintln(H)\nis_isomorphic(apex(G), umbrella_diagram())\n\nOpenInfluenceDiagram(4 variables, 2 mechanisms, 1 decisions, 1 utilities; [:Season] -> [:Weather])\n\ntrue\n\notimes (or ⊗) places diagrams side by side, concatenating the interfaces and their flags:\n\nT = A ⊗ B\nprintln(T)\ndom(T) == dom(A) ⊗ dom(B), codom(T) == codom(A) ⊗ codom(B)\n\nOpenInfluenceDiagram(5 variables, 2 mechanisms, 1 decisions, 1 utilities; [:Weather, :Forecast!] -> [:Weather, :Forecast!])\n\n(true, true)\n\noapply composes along an undirected wiring diagram written with @relation. Each box receives a diagram, its ports are the interface variables (inputs first), and the interfaces meeting at a junction are compared, flags included, before the colimit is taken:\n\nuwd = @relation () begin\n    chance(weather, forecast)\n    decide(weather, forecast)\nend\nO = oapply(uwd, Dict(:chance => A, :decide => B))\nis_isomorphic(apex(O), umbrella_diagram())\n\ntrue\n\nThe identity on an interface is the diagram that holds the interface’s variables and nothing else; composing with it changes nothing up to isomorphism:\n\nis_isomorphic(apex(compose(A, id(codom(A)))), apex(A))\n\ntrue"},{"title":"Semantics for a fixed strategy: reward kernels","page":"Open influence diagrams and composition","location":"tutorials/01_open_influence_diagrams/#Semantics-for-a-fixed-strategy:-reward-kernels","category":"section","text":"Fix a deterministic strategy. An open diagram X → Y then denotes a reward kernel (K, W): K(y | x) is the probability of reaching the output state y from the input state x, and W(y, x) the expected reward earned on the way, weighted by the probability of reaching y. Reward kernels compose by (K₁, W₁) ; (K₂, W₂) = (K₁K₂, W₁K₂ + K₁W₂): the reward of the composite is what the first component earns, carried forward by the second component’s kernel, plus what the second earns from where the first left off. This is kernel composition over the expectation semiring (p, r)·(q, s) = (pq, ps + rq).\n\ninterpret computes the reward kernel of an open diagram from the tables, which it looks up by kernel reference or by variable name, and a strategy. The chance component has no decision, so its strategy is empty, and no utility, so its reward is zero:\n\nks = Dict(x => kernel(m, x) for x in (:Weather, :Forecast))\nus = bound_utilities(m)\nrA = interpret(A, ks, us, Strategy())\nprintln(rA)\nkernel_matrix(kernel(rA)), reward_matrix(rA)\n\nRewardKernel(Symbol[] → [:Weather, :Forecast])\n\n([0.48999999999999994; 0.045; … ; 0.06999999999999999; 0.18;;], [0.0; 0.0; … ; 0.0; 0.0;;])\n\nThe decision component, under the strategy that takes the umbrella only on a rainy forecast, is a reward kernel from the six joint states of (Weather, Forecast) to the empty interface: the probability part discards its input, and the reward part is the utility of the weather and of the action the policy picks for the forecast.\n\nσ = Strategy(:Umbrella => deterministic_policy(m, :Umbrella, [:leave, :leave, :take]))\nrB = interpret(B, ks, us, σ)\nprintln(rB)\nreward_matrix(rB)\n\nRewardKernel([:Weather, :Forecast] → Symbol[])\n\n1×6 Matrix{Float64}:\n 100.0  0.0  100.0  0.0  20.0  70.0\n\nTheir composite is a reward kernel from the empty interface to itself: probability one, and a reward equal to the expected utility of the strategy.\n\nr = compose(rA, rB)\n(probability = only(kernel_matrix(kernel(r))), reward = reward(r)[],\n expected_utility = expected_utility(m, σ))\n\n(probability = 0.9999999999999998, reward = 76.99999999999999, expected_utility = 76.99999999999999)"},{"title":"Functoriality, checked numerically","page":"Open influence diagrams and composition","location":"tutorials/01_open_influence_diagrams/#Functoriality,-checked-numerically","category":"section","text":"Functoriality says that interpreting the composite gives the composite of the interpretations. The check runs over every deterministic strategy of the umbrella decision, eight in all (a choice of action for each of the three forecasts), which all_deterministic_strategies lists from the decision component’s apex. For each, the reward kernel of compose(A, B) is compared with the composite of the two reward kernels, and its reward with the expected utility that InfluenceDiagrams.jl computes on the closed composite, instantiated as an ordinary model:\n\nstrategies = all_deterministic_strategies(apex(B))\nclosed_model = bind_utility(bind_kernel(InfluenceDiagramModel(apex(C)),\n                                        [x => ks[x] for x in (:Weather, :Forecast)]),\n                            :U => us[:U])\nchecks = map(strategies) do τ\n    whole = interpret(C, ks, us, τ)\n    (actions = Tuple(policy_table(τ[:Umbrella])),\n     functorial = whole ≈ compose(rA, interpret(B, ks, us, τ)),\n     eu = reward(whole)[],\n     oracle = expected_utility(closed_model, τ))\nend\n[(actions = c.actions, eu = round(c.eu; digits = 2)) for c in checks]\n\n8-element Vector{@NamedTuple{actions::Tuple{Symbol, Symbol, Symbol}, eu::Float64}}:\n (actions = (:take, :take, :take), eu = 35.0)\n (actions = (:leave, :take, :take), eu = 71.05)\n (actions = (:take, :leave, :take), eu = 40.95)\n (actions = (:leave, :leave, :take), eu = 77.0)\n (actions = (:take, :take, :leave), eu = 28.0)\n (actions = (:leave, :take, :leave), eu = 64.05)\n (actions = (:take, :leave, :leave), eu = 33.95)\n (actions = (:leave, :leave, :leave), eu = 70.0)\n\nEvery strategy passes both checks:\n\nall(c -> c.functorial && c.eu ≈ c.oracle, checks)\n\ntrue\n\nThe tensor is preserved too: the reward kernel of A ⊗ B is the tensor of the two, (K₁ ⊗ K₂, W₁ ⊗ K₂ + K₁ ⊗ W₂).\n\ninterpret(A ⊗ B, ks, us, σ) ≈ otimes(rA, rB)\n\ntrue\n\nSo is gluing, which is serial composition along part of an interface, and oapply, which builds the same pushout from a wiring diagram:\n\ninterpret(G, ks, us, σ) ≈ r, interpret(O, ks, us, σ) ≈ r\n\n(true, true)"},{"title":"A first look at optimisation","page":"Open influence diagrams and composition","location":"tutorials/01_open_influence_diagrams/#A-first-look-at-optimisation","category":"section","text":"The closed composite is an ordinary influence diagram, so the solvers of InfluenceDiagrams.jl apply to it. optimize hands it to them:\n\nsol = optimize(C, ks, us)\nsol.expected_utility, policy_table(sol.strategy[:Umbrella])\n\n(77.00000000000001, [:leave, :leave, :take])\n\nThe open decision component has no expected utility of its own: it has one only in a context, a law on its inputs and a value for its outputs. boundary_law gives the joint law of (Weather, Forecast) that the chance component induces, and against it contextual_optimize finds the same optimum:\n\nν = boundary_law(A, ks, us, Strategy())\nlocal_sol = contextual_optimize(B, ks, us; prior = ν)\nlocal_sol.expected_utility ≈ sol.expected_utility, local_sol.strategy == sol.strategy\n\n(true, true)\n\nThat optimising a component against the context its neighbours induce recovers the optimum of the whole, while optimising it against any other context need not, is the subject of the third vignette, Why optimisation does not compose."},{"title":"Summary","page":"Open influence diagrams and composition","location":"tutorials/01_open_influence_diagrams/#Summary","category":"section","text":"An open influence diagram is a structured cospan of influence diagrams whose interface variables are flagged observed or hidden. The typed-interface rule refuses an input with a generator, a free variable off the interface, a decision that reads a hidden input, and a composite that forgets an earlier decision; glued interfaces must agree on observability as well as on names and states. compose, glue, otimes and oapply are pushouts and coproducts of ACSets with the rule checked again on the result. For a fixed strategy, interpret sends each of them to the corresponding operation on reward kernels, and on a closed composite the reward is the expected utility of the instantiated diagram. The next vignette, Composing a management decision with a zoo network, applies the same constructions to an ecological network from the model zoo."},{"title":"References","page":"Open influence diagrams and composition","location":"tutorials/01_open_influence_diagrams/#References","category":"section","text":"<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-BaezCourser2020\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nBaez, John C., and Kenny Courser. 2020. “Structured Cospans.” Theory and Applications of Categories 35 (48): 1771–822.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Fong2012\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nFong, Brendan. 2012. Causal Theories: A Categorical Perspective on Bayesian Networks. https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.6201.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-HowardMatheson2005\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nHoward, Ronald A., and James E. Matheson. 2005. “Influence Diagrams.” Decision Analysis 2 (3): 127–43. https://doi.org/10.1287/deca.1050.0020.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"title":"CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.jl","page":"Home","location":"#CategoricalInfluenceDiagrams.jl","category":"section","text":"Open influence diagrams as structured cospans, following ADR 0018 of the ecosystem. The package is the decision-layer analogue of CategoricalBayesianNetworks.jl: it gives the influence diagrams of InfluenceDiagrams.jl open boundaries and composes them, keeping Catlab out of the model layer (ADR 0009)."},{"title":"What is here","page":"Home","location":"#What-is-here","category":"section","text":"Open influence diagrams. An OpenInfluenceDiagram is a Catlab structured cospan whose apex is an InfluenceDiagram and whose feet are VariableSpaces, the same feet as those of an open Bayesian network. Build one with Open.\nObservability. Every interface variable is :observed, remembered information that a later decision may read, or :hidden. The flags sit beside the cospan, one per interface position, rather than in the feet, so that the feet stay interchangeable with those of CategoricalBayesianNetworks.jl. Everything is hidden unless declared observed, by name on every foot (observed = [...]) or foot by foot (observed = (inputs = [...], outputs = [...])). A pass-through variable observed on the input foot and hidden on the output foot is forgotten at the boundary; hidden on the input foot and observed on the output foot, it is revealed by the component, whose own decisions still may not read it. Two interfaces that agree on variables and states but not on flags do not match (ObservabilityMismatchError).\nThe typed-interface rule, checked by validation_errors and on construction:\nevery variable has at most one generator, a chance mechanism or a decision;\nan input-foot variable has no generator in the apex;\na decision reads only internal variables and observed input-foot variables;\na utility scope may read any apex variable, inputs included;\nthe causal-plus-information graph is acyclic;\ncomposition preserves no-forgetting when the components have it: checked on every composite, reported as a NoForgettingError naming the decision and the missing information arc.\nComposition. compose, glue, otimes and oapply are pushouts or coproducts of ACSets, with the rule checked again on the composite. Decisions and utilities stay inside their components; only variables cross a boundary.\nOpen Bayesian networks embed with every interface variable hidden: OpenInfluenceDiagram(o::OpenBayesNet), inverted by OpenBayesNet(d).\nSemantics for a fixed strategy. A RewardKernel (K, W) pairs a FiniteKernel K from the input interface to the output interface with a table W of the same shape: the probability-weighted reward earned on the way. Reward kernels compose by (K₁, W₁) ; (K₂, W₂) = (K₁K₂, W₁K₂ + K₁W₂) and tensor by (K₁ ⊗ K₂, W₁ ⊗ K₂ + K₁ ⊗ W₂), with identities, copies, discards and swaps lifted from FiniteKernels.jl with zero reward (compose, otimes, identity_reward_kernel, copy_reward_kernel, discard_reward_kernel, swap_reward_kernel). Discard is not natural: dropping an output keeps the reward earned on the way to it.\nInterpretation. interpret maps an open influence diagram and a deterministic strategy to its reward kernel, in the layout CategoricalBayesianNetworks.jl uses for open networks. The strategy of a composite is the disjoint union of its components' strategies (merge_strategies). On a closed composite the result is (1, EU), with EU the expected utility that InfluenceDiagrams.jl computes, and the test suite checks interpret against that oracle under compose, glue, otimes and oapply, on every strategy of the umbrella and oil-wildcatter splits, on the SPEC §46 grazing diagram at two boundaries, and on random small diagrams.\nOptimisation, contextual and never isolated. A composite is an ordinary InfluenceDiagram, and optimize hands a closed composite to the solvers of InfluenceDiagrams.jl. An open component f : X → Y has no expected utility of its own: contextual_value scores it under a fixed strategy against an explicit prior μ on its joint input states and an explicit continuation c on its joint output states, Σ_x μ(x) Σ_y (W(y, x) + K(y | x) c(y)), and contextual_optimize maximises that over the legal deterministic strategies, by handing the component to the ExhaustivePolicySearch of InfluenceDiagrams.jl with the prior over its input variables and the continuation over its output variables as a DecisionContext (its brute-force path, through interpret, is the cross-check). For a serial composite f ⋅ g the contexts are the suffix's value function (continuation_value) and the prefix's boundary law (boundary_law), and the contextual identity EU_{f;g}(σ, τ; μ, c) = EU_f(σ; μ, c_τ) = L_σ + EU_g(τ; ν_σ, c) holds; the test suite checks it against the closed-composite oracle, and checks that both orders of contextual maximisation reach the optimum the solvers find. There is deliberately no \"optimise each component, then compose\" operation. The test suite reproduces the counterexample of the research bundle's Lean track: a fair hidden bit, an early action with utility -[A], a late action that observes only A with utilities 4[A] and 2[B = H]. Each component optimised in isolation (zero continuation, actual induced prior) composes to the value 1, while the composite attains 4. Maximising separately at each hidden boundary state would need a policy that reads the hidden input, which rule 3 rejects.\nLenses and equilibria (ADR 0020). Along a chain of components, component_contexts gives each component the prior and continuation the rest of the chain induces, regrets and is_equilibrium test whether any one component could gain alone, and best_response_dynamics revises one component at a time until none can. An equilibrium can be worth less than the optimum; see Lenses and equilibria.\nReward certificates (ADR 0019). export_reward_certificate writes a diagram, its flags and tables, a deterministic strategy and the reward kernel interpret computed for it as an OpenInfluence.RewardCertificate/v1 record, for the Lean checker in proofs/ (lake exe check_certificate). See Reward certificates."},{"title":"Proofs, and what is not here","page":"Home","location":"#Proofs,-and-what-is-not-here","category":"section","text":"The Lean project in proofs/ builds on the proof projects of CategoricalBayesianNetworks.jl and InfluenceDiagrams.jl. It proves the reward-kernel category and its symmetric monoidal structure, the functoriality of the fixed-strategy interpretation under serial composition and tensor, the contextual identity, and the two obstructions of the research bundle. Its checker for reward certificates ties Julia's interpret of a recorded diagram to that interpretation, cell by cell (ADR 0019). proofs/README.md lists the theorems and their restrictions; the test suite checks the same laws numerically.\n\nRandomised (behavioural) policies and evidence across a boundary are outside the first release. That a Julia composite is the Lean composite of its parts is not proved: a certificate checks the diagram it records, not the operation that built it."}]
}
